@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex what Harmless is
Harmless is a coding harness written in Emacs Lisp.  It is not gptel.
It does not wrap Grok Build, Claude Code, or the Codex CLI.  It talks
to a model provider itself, keeps the transcript in Emacs, and runs
tools in the project directory.

A session is one conversation.  It is pinned to one connection and one
model.  Several sessions can run in one Emacs.  One can use Grok,
another a local model, and a third Claude, each in its own project.

The transcript buffer is a view.  The saved session on disk is the
record.  Closing the buffer does not delete the session.

Harmless requires GNU Emacs 31.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@findex harmless-setup
Put the @file{lisp} directory on @code{load-path} and load the package.

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/programming/emacs/harmless/lisp")
(require 'harmless)
@end example

@vindex harmless-providers
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-provider-name
Choose the connections Harmless may use.  Most people need one.

@example
(setq harmless-providers (list (harmless-make-xai))
      harmless-default-model "grok-4.6"
      harmless-default-reasoning-effort "xhigh")
@end example

@findex harmless-make-xai
@findex harmless-make-openai
@findex harmless-make-anthropic
@findex harmless-make-openai-compat
The constructors are @code{harmless-make-xai},
@code{harmless-make-openai}, @code{harmless-make-anthropic}, and
@code{harmless-make-openai-compat}.  Each takes an optional connection
name.  The default names are @samp{xAI}, @samp{OpenAI}, and
@samp{Anthropic}.  A second account needs its own name, such as
@samp{xAI work}.

@vindex harmless-directory
Settings can live in your init file or in @file{config.el} under
@code{harmless-directory}.  That directory defaults to @file{harmless}
inside @code{user-emacs-directory}, so a typical path is
@file{~/.emacs.d/harmless/config.el}.  Harmless loads @file{config.el}
once, the first time a command needs it.

If @code{harmless-providers} is empty, @kbd{M-x harmless} asks you to
pick a provider and a connection name.

A local OpenAI-compatible server looks like this:

@example
(setq harmless-providers
      (list (harmless-make-openai-compat
             "local"
             :host "127.0.0.1:11434"
             :protocol "http"
             :endpoint "/v1/chat/completions"
             :key "none"
             :models '("llama3.2"))))
@end example

@node Starting
@chapter Starting

@findex harmless
@kindex M-x harmless
@kbd{M-x harmless} opens the session for the current project.  A
prefix argument opens the dashboard instead.

The current project is the project root Emacs would use for the
buffer, or @code{default-directory} when the buffer is not in a
project.  The directory must be absolute.  Harmless refuses to treat a
missing directory as the filesystem root.

On the first run, sign in when the provider asks.  @xref{Accounts}.

@node Sessions
@chapter Sessions

@findex harmless-current
@findex harmless-new
@findex harmless-chat
@findex harmless-switch
@findex harmless-dashboard
@cindex session
@cindex detached chat

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-current
Open or resume the newest session for this project.  If none exists,
start one with the default connection and model.

@item M-x harmless-new
Start a new session in the current project.  Harmless asks for the
connection, the model, and the reasoning effort when more than one
choice exists.

@item M-x harmless-chat
Start a chat with no project and no working directory.  This is a
detached chat.  It can use global memory and ask you a question.  It
cannot read project files, run a shell, or enter plan mode.

@item M-x harmless-switch
Jump to a live or saved session.  Completion shows the project, the
title, and the session id.

@item M-x harmless-dashboard
List every session, grouped by project.  @xref{Keys}, for the
dashboard keys.
@end table

The first user message becomes the title, cut at 60 characters, until
you set a title when you create the session.  A set title stays.

@findex harmless-rename
@findex harmless-export-session
@findex harmless-delete-session
@kindex t @r{(transcript)}
@kindex x @r{(transcript)}
@kindex X @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{t}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-rename}, sets the title.  The session
id and its directory stay.  Later prompts do not replace that title.
Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{session title is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{session title
must be a string}.

@kbd{x}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-export-session}, writes the transcript
as Markdown.  The session is not modified.  A relative path stays
inside the project and is not resolved against the buffer's default
directory.  An absolute path is written only when its parent already
exists.  @code{~} is not expanded.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{session export
path is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{session
export path must be a string}.  A path that names a directory is
refused: @samp{session export path is not a file}.  @code{~} is
refused: @samp{session export path must be absolute}.  A relative
path outside the project is refused: @samp{session export path
escapes the project: }, followed by the path.  An absolute path
whose parent is missing is refused: @samp{session export path is
missing}.  @code{/} and a drive root are refused: @samp{session
export path is the filesystem root}.  A relative path in a detached
chat, a blank project, a relative project, or a project that is the
filesystem root is refused before anything is written.

@kbd{X}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-delete-session}, deletes that session's
directory and drops it from the live list.  An interactive call asks
first.  A turn that is streaming or waiting on permission is refused:
@samp{session is busy}.  The filesystem root is not a session
directory.  Another session's files stay.

The header line of the transcript shows the title (or the project
name), the connection, the model, the status, and the word
@samp{plan} while plan mode is on.  @xref{Plan mode}.

Two sessions in one project get separate transcript buffers.  The
buffer name is @code{*harmless: @var{project}*} when the project has
one session, and @code{*harmless: @var{project} @var{id}*} when it has
more.  A detached chat always includes the id.

@node Transcript
@chapter Transcript

@findex harmless-session-mode
@kindex TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex S-TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex LEFT @r{(transcript)}
@kindex RIGHT @r{(transcript)}
The transcript is read-only.  Replies are Markdown.  A tool call is a
folded line.  A run of reads and searches folds into one
@samp{explored} line.

@table @kbd
@item TAB
Toggle the fold at point.

@item S-TAB
Toggle every fold.

@item LEFT
Close the fold at point.

@item RIGHT
Open the fold at point.

@item RET
Move to the prompt.

@item i
Move to the prompt.

@item q
Quit the window.

@item k
Compact the transcript.

@item w
Rewind to the turn at point.

@item E
Edit the prompt at point and rerun from there.

@item f
Fork through the turn at point.

@item t
Rename the session.  The id stays.

@item x
Export the transcript as Markdown.

@item X
Delete the session.
@end table

@findex harmless-compact
@vindex harmless-compact-keep-turns
@vindex harmless-compact-threshold
@kindex k @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{k}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-compact}, summarizes the older turns and
replaces them with one summary.  The recent turns stay in the
transcript.  A turn starts at a user message and includes the
assistant reply and tool results that follow it.  The default keeps
the last two turns (@code{harmless-compact-keep-turns}).  A numeric
prefix argument sets how many turns to keep, and the command keeps at
least one.  An empty summary leaves the transcript as it was.  A turn
that is streaming or waiting on permission is left alone.  The same
command is @kbd{k} in the Harmless menu.

When the last prompt has used at least @code{harmless-compact-threshold}
percent of the model's context window, the next prompt compacts first.
The default is 80.  The same percent applies to every model.  A model
with a larger window compacts at the same fullness.  Nil turns
automatic compact off.  A model with no known window is left alone.
An automatic compact that fails still sends the prompt.

@findex harmless-rewind
@kindex w @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{w}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-rewind}, drops every turn after the one
at point and saves the shorter transcript.  That turn stays, including
its assistant reply and tool results.  A numeric prefix argument keeps
that many turns from the start of the transcript.  Outside the
transcript, the command asks which turn to keep.  It keeps at least
one turn and asks before dropping anything.  The dropped turns are not
saved.  The last prompt size is cleared, and the running token totals
stay.  A turn that is streaming or waiting on permission is left
alone.  The same command is @kbd{w} in the Harmless menu.

@findex harmless-edit-prompt
@kindex E @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{E}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-edit-prompt}, replaces the prompt at point
and sends the new text.  The reply to the old prompt, and every turn
after it, is dropped and is not saved.  A numeric prefix argument
chooses the turn, counting from the start of the transcript.  Outside
the transcript, the command asks which turn to edit.  It asks before
changing anything.  The last prompt size is cleared, and the running
token totals stay.  A turn that is streaming or waiting on permission
is left alone.  The same command is @kbd{E} in the Harmless menu.

@findex harmless-fork
@kindex f @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{f}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-fork}, copies the transcript through the
turn at point into a new session.  That turn stays in the copy,
including its assistant reply and tool results.  Later turns stay in
the original session.  The original session is left unchanged.
Nothing is sent.  A numeric prefix argument chooses the turn, counting
from the start of the transcript.  Outside the transcript, the command
asks which turn to copy through.  It asks before creating the new
session.  The copy starts with empty token totals.  A turn that is
streaming or waiting on permission is left alone.  The same command is
@kbd{f} in the Harmless menu.

Click the model name in the header line to change the connection, the
model, and the effort.  @xref{Models}.

@node Prompt
@chapter Prompt

@findex harmless-prompt-send
@findex harmless-steer
@findex harmless-prompt-mode
@kindex C-c C-c
@kindex C-c RET
The prompt is a small window under the transcript.  Type there and
send the turn.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send the prompt.  @kbd{C-c @key{RET}} does the same.  While a turn is
streaming, this queues the prompt instead of aborting the turn.

@item C-c C-a
Abort the in-flight turn or shell.  @xref{Trouble}.

@item C-c C-k
Open the Harmless menu.  @xref{Keys}.
@end table

An empty prompt is refused: @samp{Prompt is empty}.  Nil, @code{""},
and whitespace are the same refusal.  While a permission question is
showing, send is refused until you answer it.  @xref{Permissions}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-steer} reads a follow-up from the minibuffer and
queues it without aborting the turn.  That includes while a permission
question is showing.  The same empty-prompt refusal applies.  A value
such as @code{/}, @code{c:/}, or @code{sub/} is text, and it is queued.

The follow-up is sent after the current tool batch, before the next
request to the model.  A reply that used no tools is followed by the
queued text, and the turn continues.  Several follow-ups keep the
order they were queued.  Abort drops a queue that has not been sent.

@node Permissions
@chapter Permissions

@findex harmless-set-permission-mode
@vindex harmless-permission-mode
@cindex permission
@kindex y @r{(permission)}
@kindex n @r{(permission)}
@kindex ! @r{(permission)}
Each tool belongs to a class: @code{read}, @code{edit},
@code{shell}, or @code{network}.  A read never asks.  An edit asks.
A shell asks.  A network call asks.

A @code{run_shell} command that Harmless can see is read-only is
treated as a read, so it does not ask.  A command Harmless does not
recognize still asks.  A redirect that writes, a command
substitution, and a subshell still ask.  @code{shell_kill} still
asks.  In plan mode a read-only shell command still runs, and a
shell command that can write is refused before the question.
@xref{Plan mode}.
A file watch does not ask.  A command that can write asks, and the
question shows that command.  @xref{Monitors}.
Scheduling a prompt asks, and the question shows that prompt.
Accepting edits does not ask.  @xref{Schedules}.

The permission mode of a session changes that.

@table @code
@item ask
The default.  Edits, shell commands, and network calls ask.

@item accept-edits
Edits proceed.  Shell commands and network calls still ask.

@item always-approve
Edits, shell commands, and network calls proceed.

@item auto
A reviewer decides an escalation.  A call that would ask is
checked first.  @code{allow} skips the question.
@code{escalate} asks.  An allow rule skips the reviewer.  A
deny, a dangerous command, and a shell write in plan mode still
refuse before the reviewer.  @code{ask}, @code{accept-edits},
and @code{always-approve} do not call the reviewer.  A read
still does not ask.
@end table

@kbd{M-x harmless-set-permission-mode} sets the mode for the current
session.  The menu key is @kbd{p}.  New sessions take the value of
@code{harmless-permission-mode}, which defaults to @code{ask}.

When a tool needs a decision, the prompt shows the class and a short
description of the call.  For an edit, that description is the path.
For a shell command, it is the command.  For a web search, it is the
query.  For a fetched page, it is the URL.  For an X search, it
is the query or the post id.  For an MCP call, it is the resource
uri or the server name.  For a click, it is the point.  For typed
text, it is the text.  For a key, it is the key.  For a browser,
it is the URL.  A screenshot shows the tool name.  A file
watch does not ask.  A command that can write shows the command.
A scheduled prompt shows the prompt.  Cancelling one shows the id.

@table @kbd
@item y
Allow this call.

@item n
Deny this call.  The model receives an error and can try something else.

@item !
Allow this class for the rest of the session.
@end table

Those three keys insert themselves when no question is pending, so you
can type them in an ordinary prompt.

A denied call does not change the project.  An allowed edit or shell
command runs at once.

@vindex harmless-permission-rules
@vindex harmless-permission-denies
@code{harmless-permission-rules} and
@code{harmless-permission-denies} are lists of rules copied onto a
session when it is created and when it is loaded.  A rule set only
on that session is not saved, and loading the session replaces it
with these lists.

@file{.harmless/permissions} in the project is an allow list that
survives the session.  One rule per line.  A blank line is
ignored.  A line that starts with @code{#} is a comment.  Those
rules are copied onto every session in that project, including
one that is loaded again.  A detached chat has no project file.
Nil, @code{""}, a relative path, and the filesystem root are not
a project.  A missing file adds no rules and creates nothing.  A
directory at that path is refused: @samp{permissions file is a
directory}.  A bad line is refused with the file path, a colon,
and the rule error.  The file is not copied into the session,
and saving the session does not write it.

A rule is @code{Bash(PATTERN)}, @code{Edit(PATTERN)}, or
@code{WebFetch(domain:HOST)}.  @code{Bash(npm run *)} matches a
shell command, and the same form matches a command monitor.
@code{*} matches any characters, including spaces and slashes.
A file watch is not a command.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are
command patterns, not paths.

@code{Edit(/src/**)} matches a path inside the project.
@code{*} matches one path segment.  @code{**} matches across
segments.  A leading slash is the project, not the filesystem
root.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, a drive prefix, @code{..}, and
@code{~} are refused when the rule is read.
@code{WebFetch(domain:example.com)} matches that host and a
subdomain.  It does not match a search.

A deny is checked before the question.  It wins over an allow,
and it still refuses in @code{accept-edits} and
@code{always-approve}, including a read that would not have
asked.  The model receives @samp{Error: Permission rule denied },
followed by the rule.  An allow skips the question when every
command, path, or host matches.  A patch is allowed only when
every hunk path matches.  One denied hunk refuses the whole
patch, and nothing is written.  Plan mode still refuses a shell
command that can write, before these rules.  A read-only command
that plan mode allows can still be denied.

@vindex harmless-dangerous-commands
@code{harmless-dangerous-commands} names programs that are refused
before the question, including in @code{always-approve}.  An allow
rule does not override the list.  The match is the lower-case file
name.  @code{sudo} and @code{doas} are unwrapped once.  A name that
starts with @code{mkfs.} is refused.  @code{rm} of @code{/}, a drive
root, @code{~}, or @code{/*} is refused too.  A longer path is not.
The model receives @samp{Error: dangerous command refused: },
followed by the program name.  In plan mode a command that can
write is still refused as a plan-mode write.

@vindex harmless-auto-review-function
In @code{auto} mode the reviewer runs only when the call would
ask and no allow rule matches.  @code{harmless-auto-review-function},
when set, is that classifier.  It is called with the session,
the tool, and the arguments, and it returns @code{allow} or
@code{escalate}.  Any other return, or an error, escalates.
Nil asks the reviewer model.  The model sees the tool name and
the arguments and is given no tools.  Only a reply that is
exactly @code{allow} or @code{escalate} is kept.  A missing
provider, an error, a tool call, or any other reply escalates.
The reviewer does not run the tool.  The classifier is not saved
on the session.  The mode is saved.  @kbd{M-x
harmless-set-permission-mode} accepts @code{auto}.  Nil uses the
default mode.  An unknown mode is refused: @samp{permission mode
is not a mode}.  A saved mode Harmless does not know is loaded
as @code{ask}.

@vindex harmless-hooks
@code{harmless-hooks} is a list of hooks.  It is not saved on the
session.  A @code{pre} hook can block a tool call before the
question.  The model receives @samp{Error: hook blocked this tool
call}, or that text, a colon, and the reason.  A block does not
run the tool.  A hook cannot override plan mode, the
dangerous-command list, or a deny rule, and a hook cannot skip
the question.  A @code{post} hook runs after the tool and can
replace the result the model sees.  The files the tool wrote stay
as they are.  A hook error blocks before the call and leaves the
result it was given after the call.  A hook is a function, a
command, an HTTP request, an MCP call, a prompt, or a subagent.
Nil, @code{""}, @code{/}, and @code{c:/} are not URLs and are not
fetched.

@node Accounts
@chapter Accounts

@findex harmless-login
@findex harmless-logout
@kindex M-x harmless-login
@kindex M-x harmless-logout
@kbd{M-x harmless-login} signs in.  If several connections exist, it
asks which one.  @kbd{M-x harmless-logout} forgets the Harmless token
for that connection.  It does not delete a token that belongs to Grok
Build, Claude Code, or Codex.

@heading xAI

@vindex harmless-xai-use-grok-auth
Browser login opens @code{auth.x.ai}.  Harmless listens on port 56121.
Tokens are stored under @code{harmless-directory}, mode 0600, and
refresh on their own.  A prefix argument uses the device-code flow.
Harmless also uses that flow when port 56121 is already taken, for
example by Grok Build.

If you have already run @code{grok login} and
@code{harmless-xai-use-grok-auth} is non-nil, Harmless reuses
@file{~/.grok/auth.json} until you sign in or out from Emacs.  It
never writes that file.  A refresh is saved under
@code{harmless-directory} instead.

An API key still works when no OAuth session is active.  Put it in
@file{~/.authinfo} as @code{machine api.x.ai login apikey password
xai-...}, or export @code{XAI_API_KEY}.

@heading OpenAI

@vindex harmless-openai-use-codex-auth
Login opens ChatGPT OAuth and listens on @code{localhost:1455}.  A
prefix argument asks you to paste the redirect URL.  Harmless also
does that when port 1455 is busy.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-openai.json}.  A still-valid @file{~/.codex/auth.json} is
reused and never written.

ChatGPT subscription tokens are sent to the Codex Responses API, not
to @code{api.openai.com}.  @code{OPENAI_API_KEY} remains a usage-based
fallback on Chat Completions.

@heading Anthropic

@vindex harmless-anthropic-use-claude-auth
Login uses Claude Code's OAuth client.  The browser shows a code.
Paste it at the minibuffer prompt.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  A still-valid
@file{~/.claude/.credentials.json} access token is reused.  Harmless
does not write or refresh that file.

@heading Several accounts

Give each account its own connection name.  The default names keep the
original files: @file{auth.json}, @file{auth-openai.json}, and
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  Any other name gets
@file{auth-@var{slug}.json}.  A session stays on the connection it was
given.  One buffer can use a personal Grok account while another uses
a work Claude account.

A local server has no browser login.

@node Models
@chapter Models

@findex harmless-pick-model
@findex harmless-set-model
@findex harmless-set-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@cindex reasoning effort
Click the model in the header line, or run @kbd{M-x harmless-pick-model}.
Harmless asks for a logged-in connection, then a model, then a
reasoning effort.  A provider with one model skips the model prompt.
A model that does not take an effort skips the effort prompt.

You can also set the pieces separately.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-model}
changes the model.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-reasoning-effort} changes
the effort.  An empty effort clears the session's own value and falls
back to @code{harmless-default-reasoning-effort}.

Models whose names start with @code{grok-4}, @code{gpt-5}, or
@code{gpt-6}, and Claude models, accept an effort.  The usual values
are @code{low}, @code{medium}, @code{high}, and @code{xhigh}.  Claude
also accepts @code{max}.  A model id such as @code{grok-4.6-xhigh}
sets both the model and the effort.

Nil for the default effort omits the parameter.  For grok-4.6, the
provider then uses @code{high}.

The choice is saved with the session.  The next turn uses it.

@node Tools
@chapter Tools

@cindex tools
The model does not change your files by printing a diff.  It calls
tools.  You see each call in the transcript.  Permission rules are in
@ref{Permissions}.

Tool calls in one reply run together.  Harmless asks for permission
one call at a time.  An allowed call starts before the next question,
so several shell commands overlap.  Results are added to the
transcript in the order the model requested them.  Abort cancels the
calls that are still running.  A review reads the working tree, a
base branch, or one commit and records findings.  It does not edit
the project.  @xref{Review}.
@code{lsp} asks a language server that is already running for a
definition, references, or diagnostics.  @xref{Language server}.
@code{read_file} also reads an image, a PDF, and a notebook.
@code{notebook_edit} replaces one notebook cell.  @kbd{I} attaches
an image to the next prompt.  @xref{File tools}.
@code{skill} loads one skill into later turns.  @xref{Skills}.
@code{generate_image} writes a generated image into the project.
@xref{Image generation}.

Paths are relative to the project root unless you pass an absolute
path that is still inside that root, or inside a directory added for
this session.  A path that escapes both is refused.  An empty path is
refused.  The filesystem root is not a project: a search there is
refused, and Harmless will not store its own state there.

@findex harmless-add-directory
@kindex + @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{+}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-add-directory}, lets this session read
and edit one directory outside the project.  The same command is
@kbd{+} in the Harmless menu.  The directory is saved with the
session.  A fork and a subagent keep the list.  @code{glob} searches
the project and every added directory.  With no @code{path},
@code{grep} does too.  The shell's remembered directory is still the
project only.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{extra directory
is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{extra
directory must be a string}.  A relative path, including @code{~},
is refused and is not expanded: @samp{extra directory must be
absolute}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a drive root are refused:
@samp{extra directory is the filesystem root}.  A missing path is
refused: @samp{extra directory is missing}.  A file is refused:
@samp{extra directory is not a directory}.  The project itself, and
a directory inside it, are refused: @samp{extra directory is inside
the project}.  A trailing slash is the same directory.  A detached
chat is refused: @samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.

A path outside the project and outside every added directory is
still refused: @samp{Path escapes project: }, followed by the path.

A detached chat is offered @code{memory_read},
@code{memory_remember}, @code{memory_write_topic}, and
@code{ask_user}.  @xref{Memory}.  @xref{Questions}.
It can list live sessions and send one a finding.
@xref{Messages}.
It can search the web and fetch a page.  @xref{Web search}.
@xref{Fetch a page}.
It can search X for posts, a thread, and accounts.
@xref{X search}.
It can list and read MCP resources.  @xref{MCP}.
It can see the screen, click, type, and open a browser.
@xref{Computer use}.
It can schedule a prompt.  @xref{Schedules}.
It can keep a todo list.  @xref{Todos}.

@menu
* File tools::  Read, list, write, replace, patch, and notebooks.
* Search::      Find files and lines.
* Shell::       Run a command.
* Questions::   Ask one question with choices.
* Subagents::   A child session that reports a summary.
* Workflows::   One script, many child sessions, one result.
* Worktrees::   Isolated checkouts of the same repository.
* Messages::    A finding sent to another live session.
* Web search::  Look up a query on the web.
* Fetch a page::  Read one http or https page.
* X search::    Find posts, a thread, and accounts on X.
* MCP::         Call a configured server and read its resources.
* Computer use::  See the screen, click, type, and open a browser.
* Monitors::    Wake when a line in a file or a stream matches.
* Schedules::   Send one prompt at a later time.
* Todos::       A list of work with status and dependencies.
* Review::      A read-only review.  Findings are not applied.
* Language server::  Definition, references, and diagnostics.
@end menu

@node File tools
@section File tools

@code{read_file}, @code{list_dir}, @code{write_file}, @code{replace},
and @code{apply_patch} also accept a path inside a directory added
with @kbd{M-x harmless-add-directory}.

@table @code
@item read_file
Read a project file.  @code{offset} is the first line, counting from
1.  @code{limit} is how many lines to return.  Those arguments apply
only to a text file.  The result is cut at
@code{harmless-read-file-max-bytes} (100000).  A cut text result ends
with @samp{[truncated]}.  A read does not change the file.

An image is recognized by its bytes, not by its name.  The result is
the media type, the path, the byte count, and the bytes.  A file
larger than the cap is refused: @samp{image is too large}.

A PDF is a file whose name ends in @code{.pdf}.  Harmless reads
literal strings from the file.  It does not render the page.  A file
that does not begin with the PDF header is refused: @samp{pdf is not
a pdf}.  No literal string is @samp{No text}.  A long PDF is cut at
the same byte cap and ends with @samp{[truncated]}.

A notebook is a file whose name ends in @code{.ipynb}.  The result
lists each cell.  No cells is @samp{No cells}.  A file that is not a
notebook is refused: @samp{notebook is not a notebook}.  A notebook
larger than the cap is refused: @samp{notebook is too large}.

@item notebook_edit
Replace the source of one cell in an existing @code{.ipynb} file.
@code{path} is the notebook.  @code{cell} is a cell id or a 0-based
index.  @code{source} is the new source.  An empty source clears the
cell.  Other cells stay.  The file is not created.  This is an edit.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace for the path are refused:
@samp{notebook path is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{notebook path must be a string}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a
drive root are refused and are not expanded: @samp{notebook path is
the filesystem root}.  A missing notebook is refused: @samp{notebook
is missing}.  A directory and a trailing slash are refused:
@samp{notebook is not a notebook}.

A missing cell is refused: @samp{notebook cell is empty}.  A value
that is not a cell id or a 0-based index is refused: @samp{notebook
cell is not a cell}.  No such cell is refused: @samp{notebook cell is
missing}.  A missing source is refused: @samp{notebook source is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{notebook source
must be a string}.

@findex harmless-attach-image
@findex notebook_edit
@kindex I @r{(Harmless menu)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-attach-image} queues an image on the next prompt.
The menu key is @kbd{I}.  The file must already exist.  Harmless
reads it and does not change it.  The queue is not saved.  Sending
the prompt stores the image on that user message.  An empty prompt
is allowed when an image is queued.  A steer is still text.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{image path is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{image path must
be a string}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a drive root are refused
and are not expanded: @samp{image path is the filesystem root}.  A
relative path in a detached chat, and a path that starts with
@code{~}, are refused and are not expanded: @samp{image path must be
absolute}.  A missing file is refused: @samp{image is missing}.  A
directory, a trailing slash, and bytes that are not an image are
refused: @samp{image is not an image}.  A file larger than the cap
is refused: @samp{image is too large}.

@item list_dir
List the names in a directory.  A directory name ends with a slash.
The default path is the project root.

@item write_file
Create or replace a file with the given contents.  This is an edit.

@item replace
Replace one unique string in a file.  If the string occurs more than
once, the call fails unless @code{replace_all} is true.  This is an
edit.

@item apply_patch
Apply several hunks in one call.  Each hunk names a @code{path}, an
@code{old_string}, and a @code{new_string}.  The old string must occur
once in that file.  Later hunks on the same file see earlier
replacements.  If any hunk fails, no file is changed.  This is an edit.

In plan mode, @code{write_file}, @code{replace}, @code{apply_patch},
and @code{notebook_edit} fail.  The model must write @file{plan.md}
with @code{write_plan}.
@xref{Plan mode}.
@end table

@node Search
@section Search

@table @code
@item glob
Find files whose names match a glob.  The pattern may be a basename
or a relative path.  The search covers the project and every added
directory.  A match outside the project is an absolute path.

@item grep
Search file contents for a regular expression.  The optional
arguments are @code{path}, @code{glob}, @code{engine}, @code{context},
@code{type}, and @code{multiline}.
@end table

@cindex ripgrep
@cindex ag
@cindex grep
@code{path} is a directory inside the project or an added directory.
With no path, the search covers the project and every added
directory.  A trailing slash is the same directory.  @code{glob}
limits basenames, for example @code{*.el}.  A hit outside the
project is an absolute path.

@code{engine} is @code{rg}, @code{ag}, or @code{elisp}.  With no
engine, Harmless uses ripgrep if @command{rg} is on @code{exec-path},
then ag if @command{ag} is on @code{exec-path}, then an Emacs Lisp
scan.

Ripgrep and ag skip ignored files and the @file{.git} directory.
They do search hidden files, so a file such as @file{.harmless} is
visible.  The Emacs Lisp scan reads every regular file under the
directory, including ignored files and @file{.git}.

@code{context} is the number of lines to show around each hit.  A hit
is printed as @code{path:line:text}.  A context line is printed as
@code{path-line-text}.

@code{type} is a file type that the chosen engine knows.  Ripgrep
types come from @code{rg --type-list}.  Ag types come from
@code{ag --list-file-types}.  The Emacs Lisp scan has no file types.

@code{multiline} lets the pattern cross lines.  Only ripgrep can do
that.  Ag refuses it.  The Emacs Lisp scan refuses it.

The pattern is required, and it is case-sensitive.  An ag pattern
cannot start with a hyphen.  A long result is cut at the same byte
cap as @code{read_file}.

@node Shell
@section Shell

@vindex harmless-shell-timeout-seconds
@vindex harmless-shell-output-max-bytes
@code{run_shell} runs one command in the project root through your
shell.  The next command starts in the same directory when a
@code{cd} stayed inside the project.  Nil and @code{""} mean the
project root.  A @code{cd} that leaves the project is not kept.
Harmless reports @samp{The shell left the project. The next command starts in },
followed by the directory it kept.  The filesystem root is not kept.
The remembered directory is saved with the session.

@code{timeout} is how many seconds to wait.  When it is omitted,
@code{harmless-shell-timeout-seconds} (120) is used.  A negative
number, zero, or a value that is not a number is refused:
@samp{Error: timeout must be a positive number}.  An empty command is
refused: @samp{Error: command is empty}.  When the command is still
running at the end of that wait, Harmless leaves the process running
and returns a background id with the output captured so far.  It does
not kill the process.  @code{background} true returns that id
immediately.

@code{shell_output} reads one background shell.  @code{id} is the id
@code{run_shell} returned.  Without @code{timeout}, the tool returns
the output already available.  With @code{timeout}, it waits that many
seconds for the command to exit, then returns whatever it has.  This
tool does not ask for permission.  An empty id is refused:
@samp{Error: shell id is empty}.  An unknown id is refused:
@samp{Error: no background shell }, followed by that id.  A bad
timeout uses the same refusal as @code{run_shell}.

@code{shell_kill} stops a background shell.  It asks for permission
like any other shell command.  Killing a shell that is not running is
refused: @samp{Error: shell }, the id, and @samp{ is not running}.

The text returned to the model is cut at
@code{harmless-shell-output-max-bytes} (20000).

Abort stops a shell that is still in the foreground.  A shell that has
moved to the background keeps running.  When it exits, or when it is
killed, Emacs shows a message and a note in the transcript.  That note
is not a message the model sees on the next turn.  The jobs are kept
in memory for this Emacs session.  They are not written to the session
files.

A command Harmless recognizes as read-only does not ask for
permission.  A command it does not recognize still asks.  A redirect
that writes, a command substitution, and a subshell ask.
@xref{Permissions}.

In plan mode, a shell command that can write is refused before any
permission question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can write is refused}.
Nil, @code{""}, and a blank command are not that refusal.  A
read-only command still runs.  @code{shell_kill} still asks.
@xref{Plan mode}.

@node Questions
@section Questions

@cindex question
@code{ask_user} asks one question.  @code{question} is the prompt.
@code{choices} are the answers.  Emacs asks with completion, and the
answer must be one of those choices.  The tool returns
@samp{The user chose: } followed by that answer.

A missing or blank question is refused.  A missing choice list, an
empty list, a blank choice, a choice that spans lines, and a repeated
choice are refused.  A choice is text.  A value such as @code{/} or
@code{lisp/} is returned as that text.  Cancelling the prompt returns
@samp{The user cancelled the question.}

The tool does not ask for permission first.  A detached chat can use
it.  Plan mode can use it.

@node Subagents
@section Subagents

@cindex subagent
@cindex spawn_agent
@vindex harmless-agent-max-turns
@code{spawn_agent} starts a child session and waits for its last
reply.  The child has its own transcript.  It does not see the
parent's messages.  @code{prompt} is the task.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{description}
is an optional one-line title.  A value such as @code{/} or
@code{sub/} is a title, not a path.  @code{worktree} is an optional
checkout name.  The parent project stays where it is.  The child
edits that checkout.  @xref{Worktrees}.

@code{agent} is an optional definition name.  It is not a path.
@code{""} is refused: @samp{agent name is empty}.  @code{sub/},
@code{/}, and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{agent name is not a
name}.  A name with no definition is refused: @samp{no agent named },
followed by that name.  @code{sub} is a name.  With no file it is
@samp{no agent named sub}.

Harmless reads @file{NAME.md} from these directories, at every level
from your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem
root.  A definition closer to the session replaces one of the same
name farther away.  In one directory, later entries in this list
replace earlier ones:

@enumerate
@item
@file{.agents/agents/}

@item
@file{.codex/agents/}

@item
@file{.claude/agents/}

@item
@file{.grok/agents/}

@item
@file{.harmless/agents/}
@end enumerate

A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter:

@example
---
name: reviewer
description: Reviews code
tools: read_file, grep
model: grok-4.6
sandbox: read-only
---
Report findings.  Do not edit files.
@end example

@code{tools} limits the child.  An unknown tool name is refused.
@code{spawn_agent} cannot be one of them.  @code{model} replaces the
parent's model.  @code{inherit} keeps it.  @code{sandbox} is
@code{workspace} or @code{read-only}.  Anything else is refused:
@samp{sandbox must be workspace or read-only}.  The body is extra
instructions for that child.  Each turn lists the available agents
for the parent.  A subagent does not receive that list.

A read-only child may read files and run a shell command that cannot
write.  A tool that can write is refused before the permission
question: @samp{This subagent is read-only. A tool that can write is
refused}.  A blank shell command is not that refusal.  A read-only
child may send a finding to another live session.  @xref{Messages}.
It may also search the web.  @xref{Web search}.
It may fetch a page.  @xref{Fetch a page}.
It may search X.  @xref{X search}.
It may list and read MCP resources.  @xref{MCP}.
A read-only child is not offered computer control.
@xref{Computer use}.
It may watch a file or a read-only command.  A command that can
write is refused.  @xref{Monitors}.
A read-only child is not offered a scheduled prompt.  A workspace
child may schedule one.  @xref{Schedules}.
A workspace child uses the parent's permission mode.

In plan mode, a subagent that can write is refused before the
permission question, including when the mode is @code{always-approve}:
@samp{Plan mode is on. A subagent that can write is refused}.  A
read-only subagent still runs.  A worktree is refused even for a
read-only child:
@samp{Plan mode is on. A worktree is refused}.

The child cannot start another subagent: @samp{a subagent cannot
start another subagent}.  A tool outside its list is refused:
@samp{this subagent cannot use }, followed by the tool name.

@code{harmless-agent-max-turns} (8) is how many provider calls the
child may make.  The next call stops it: @samp{subagent stopped after
}, the number, and @samp{ turns}.  The value must be a positive
integer.

Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The parent
does not record a summary for that call.  Aborting only the child
returns @samp{subagent aborted}.  An empty child reply is @samp{The
model returned an empty reply.}

The child's sandbox, tool list, and extra prompt are saved with the
session.  The running callback is not.  A detached chat can start a
child with no definition.  A named definition needs a project
directory.

@node Workflows
@section Workflows

@cindex workflow
@cindex run_workflow
@vindex harmless-workflow-max-agents
@code{run_workflow} runs one script.  The script starts child
sessions.  The tool returns one string, the value of
@code{complete}.  The parent transcript does not gain a message for
each child.  Pass @code{name} or @code{script}, not both.  Neither
is refused: @samp{workflow needs a name or a script}.  Both are
refused: @samp{workflow takes a name or a script}.

@code{script} is the text of a script written for this task.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{workflow script must be a
string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{workflow
script is empty}.  The script is not Emacs Lisp.
Harmless does not evaluate it.  A form it does not know is refused:
@samp{unknown workflow expression}.  Text the reader cannot read is
refused: @samp{workflow script is invalid}.

@code{name} is a fixed script, not a path.  @code{""} is refused:
@samp{workflow name is empty}.  @code{sub/}, @code{/}, and
@code{c:/} are refused: @samp{workflow name is not a name}.  A name
with no file is refused: @samp{no workflow named }, followed by that
name.  @code{sub} is a name.  With no file it is @samp{no workflow
named sub}.  @code{description} is an optional one-line title.  A
value such as @code{/} or @code{sub/} is a title, not a path.

Harmless reads @file{NAME.hw} from these directories, at every level
from your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem
root.  A script closer to the session replaces one of the same name
farther away.  In one directory, later entries in this list replace
earlier ones:

@enumerate
@item
@file{.agents/workflows/}

@item
@file{.codex/workflows/}

@item
@file{.claude/workflows/}

@item
@file{.grok/workflows/}

@item
@file{.harmless/workflows/}
@end enumerate

A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter.  The body is the script:

@example
---
name: review
description: Review the diff
---
(let security (agent "Review security" :sandbox read-only))
(let tests (agent "Review tests" :sandbox read-only))
(complete (join "\n" security tests))
@end example

@code{name} in the frontmatter is the script name.  When it is
empty, the file name is the name.  A file that does not parse is
left out of the list.  Asking for it still reports the error.

The forms are @code{let} and @code{complete}.  @code{complete} is
required and must be last.  A script with none is refused:
@samp{workflow has no complete}.  A form after it is refused:
@samp{workflow has forms after complete}.  @code{let} binds a name
to an expression.  Binding it again is refused.  A name such as
@code{sub/} is refused: @samp{workflow variable is not a name}.

An expression is a string, a bound name, @code{agent},
@code{parallel}, or @code{join}.  @code{(agent PROMPT)} starts one
child.  @code{PROMPT} may be an expression.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{:sandbox} is
@code{read-only} or @code{workspace}.  The default is
@code{workspace}.  Anything else is refused: @samp{sandbox must be
workspace or read-only}.  @code{:agent} is an optional definition
name, the same names as @code{spawn_agent}.  @code{:worktree} is an
optional checkout name.  It counts as a write even when the child is
read-only.  @xref{Worktrees}.  @code{:label} and
@code{:description} are optional one-line titles.  @code{(parallel
(agent ...) ...)} starts those children together and keeps their
replies in that order.  An empty list is refused.  @code{(join SEP
EXPR ...)} joins text with @code{SEP}, which must be a string.  A
list of replies is joined the same way.  @code{complete} of a list
joins it with newlines, so the tool still returns one string.

@code{harmless-workflow-max-agents} (8) is how many children one
script may start.  More than that is refused before the first
child: @samp{workflow agent budget is }, followed by the limit.  The
value must be a positive integer.

A read-only child is one whose @code{:sandbox} is @code{read-only},
or whose definition is read-only.  A workflow whose children are all
read-only, and that starts no worktree, does not ask for permission.
A workflow that can write asks, unless the permission mode already
allows edits.  In plan
mode, a workflow that can write is refused before the permission
question, including when the mode is @code{always-approve}:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A workflow that can write is refused}.
A script that does not parse is not that refusal.  A read-only
workflow still runs.

A subagent cannot run a workflow: @samp{a subagent cannot start a
workflow}.  A definition that lists @code{run_workflow} is refused
the same way.  The child cannot start a workflow or another
subagent.

Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The
workflow's result is not added.  A detached chat can run an inline
script.  A named script needs a project directory.  Nil, @code{""},
@code{sub}, @code{sub/}, and @code{/} are not directories Harmless
searches.

@node Worktrees
@section Worktrees

@cindex worktree
@cindex enter_worktree
@cindex exit_worktree
@findex harmless-worktree
@findex harmless-worktree-exit
@code{enter_worktree} points this session at an isolated checkout of
the same repository.  @code{name} is required.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{worktree name is empty}.  @code{sub/},
@code{/}, and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{worktree name is not a
name}.  @code{sub} is a name.  A value such as @code{/} passed as
@code{description} on another tool is not a worktree name.

The checkout is created with git, under @code{harmless-directory},
outside the project.  The branch is @code{harmless/} plus the name,
starting at the session's current @code{HEAD}.  Files that are not
committed are not copied.  The same name is the same checkout.  A
second session that enters it sees the files already there.  Two
different names are two checkouts: a write in one does not change
the other, and neither write changes the original project.

Harmless will not store a checkout at the filesystem root, at a
relative path, or inside the project.  Nil, @code{""}, @code{sub},
@code{sub/}, and @code{/} are not a project:
@samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.  A drive root is not
@file{/}.  A directory that is not a git working tree is refused:
@samp{this project is not a git repository}.  When git itself cannot
be run: @samp{git is not available}.  A branch that already exists
is refused: @samp{worktree branch already exists: harmless/},
followed by the name.  A file already at the checkout path is
refused: @samp{worktree path already exists}.

Entering sets the session's project to that checkout and clears a
remembered shell directory.  The session files stay in the directory
they already used.  @kbd{M-x harmless-worktree} asks for the name.
@code{exit_worktree}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-worktree-exit}, points the
session back at the project it left.  The checkout stays on disk.  A
session that was not entered this way is refused:
@samp{this session is not in a worktree}.  A stored return path that
is missing, relative, empty, or @file{/} is refused:
@samp{worktree original is not a directory}, and the session stays
where it is.

The tool is an edit.  Ask mode asks before creating the checkout.
Accept-edits and always-approve do not ask.  In plan mode the
checkout is refused before the question, including when the mode is
@code{always-approve}:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A worktree is refused}.  Leaving does
not create a checkout, so plan mode allows it.

@code{spawn_agent} takes the same @code{worktree} name.  The child
edits the new checkout.  The parent stays in its project.  A child
born in a checkout has that checkout as its project; it was not
moved there, so leaving says it is not in a worktree.  A read-only
child still cannot write, and it is not offered @code{enter_worktree}.
Creating the checkout is a write, so plan mode refuses it even for
that child.  A workflow agent accepts @code{:worktree}.  That script
can write.  A fork keeps the checkout the session is in.  It does
not create another one, and it can leave.  Aborting a turn does not
delete a checkout.

@node Messages
@section Messages

@cindex send_message
@cindex list_sessions
@code{list_sessions} lists the sessions that are live in this Emacs.
Each line is the id, the status, and the title.  A session that is
only on disk is not listed.  Nil and an empty registry still return
the heading @samp{Live sessions:}.

@code{send_message} sends @code{text} to the live session whose id is
@code{session}.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused:
@samp{session id is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{session id is not an id}.  A missing or non-text finding is
refused: @samp{message must be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace
are refused: @samp{message is empty}.  The sender is refused:
@samp{a session cannot message itself}.  An id that is not live,
including one that was closed, is refused: @samp{no live session}
followed by that id.  @code{sub}, @code{sub/}, @code{/}, and
@code{c:/} are ids, not paths.

The finding is a line in the other session's transcript.  It does not
start a turn, and it does not change that session's title.  A session
that is idle receives it immediately: @samp{Delivered to }, followed
by the id.  A session that is streaming or waiting on permission
keeps it in memory until the transcript can take a line:
@samp{Queued for }, followed by the id.  That line is written before
the next provider call, or when the turn becomes idle or ends in an
error.  Aborting the turn writes a finding that was still queued.
Closing Emacs before that write drops it.  The finding is not a task,
and it is not kept for a session that later replaces this one.

The tool asks in @code{ask} mode.  @code{accept-edits} allows it.
Plan mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may send one.  A
detached chat may list sessions and send a finding.

@node Web search
@section Web search

@cindex web_search
@cindex network
@code{web_search} looks up @code{query} on the web.  The query is
sent to DuckDuckGo over https.  Nothing is written into the project.
Nil is refused: @samp{query must be a string}.  @code{""} and
whitespace are refused: @samp{query is empty}.  A value such as
@code{/} or @code{c:/} is text to search for, not a path.

Each hit is a title, a URL, and a snippet.  No hits returns
@samp{No results}.  At most eight hits are returned.  The same URL
is listed once.

The tool's class is @code{network}.  It asks in @code{ask} mode and
in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may search.  A
detached chat may search.  A failed request is
@samp{web search failed}.

@node Fetch a page
@section Fetch a page

@cindex web_fetch
@code{web_fetch} reads one page at @code{url}.  Only @code{http} and
@code{https} are accepted.  Nothing is written into the project.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{url is empty}.
A value that is not text is refused: @samp{url must be a string}.
A path, another scheme, or a URL with a space is refused:
@samp{url must be http or https}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/},
@code{sub}, and @code{file:///} are not pages.  A scheme without
@code{://} is refused too.

HTML is reduced to text.  A script, a style sheet, and a comment
are dropped.  A link keeps its label.  A body that contains an
HTML tag is reduced the same way.  Other text is returned as
text.  Surrounding whitespace is dropped.  An empty page returns
@samp{Empty page}.  A page longer than 100000 characters is cut
and ends with @samp{[truncated]}.

The tool's class is @code{network}.  It asks in @code{ask} mode and
in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may fetch a page.  A
detached chat may fetch a page.  A failed request is
@samp{web request failed}.

@node X search
@section X search

@cindex x_keyword_search
@cindex x_thread_fetch
@cindex x_user_search
@code{x_keyword_search} finds recent posts on X.  The search covers
the last 7 days.  @code{query} is the text, including search
operators.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are text, not paths.
@code{mode} is @code{Top} or @code{Latest}.  Latest is the default.
Top asks X for relevancy.  Latest asks for the newest posts.
@code{from_date} and @code{to_date} are @code{YYYY-MM-DD}.
@code{to_date} is the day after the last day you want.  A date
that is not that shape is refused: @samp{date must be YYYY-MM-DD}.
A start after the end is refused:
@samp{from_date is after to_date}.

Nil is refused: @samp{query must be a string}.  @code{""} and
whitespace are refused: @samp{query is empty}.  An unknown mode is
refused: @samp{mode must be Top or Latest}.  A limit that is not a
whole number is refused: @samp{limit must be a positive integer}.
A limit outside 1 to 10 is refused:
@samp{limit must be from 1 to 10}.  The default limit is 8.

Each post is a handle, a name, a time, a URL, and the text.  No
posts returns @samp{No posts}.

@code{x_thread_fetch} reads @code{post_id} and the posts around it.
Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{post id is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{post id must
be a string}.  Anything that is not a decimal id, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{post id is not an id}.
The result is at most 8 posts, oldest first, and it includes the
requested post.  No posts returns @samp{No posts}.

@code{x_user_search} finds accounts by name, handle, or bio.  The
query and limit refusals match post search.  Each account is a
name, a handle, a URL, and a bio.  No accounts returns
@samp{No users}.

The bearer token is @code{harmless-x-bearer}, or the environment
variable @code{X_BEARER_TOKEN}, or an auth-source entry for host
@code{api.x.com} and user @code{apikey}.  A missing token is
refused before a request: @samp{X is not signed in}.

The tools' class is @code{network}.  They ask in @code{ask} mode
and in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.
Plan mode does not refuse them.  A read-only subagent may search
X.  A detached chat may search X.  A response that is not JSON is
@samp{X search failed}.  A request that does not succeed is
@samp{web request failed}.  Nothing is written into the project.

@node MCP
@section MCP

@cindex MCP
@cindex mcp_list_resources
@cindex mcp_read_resource
Harmless calls tools on a local MCP server and reads that server's
resources.  Servers are named in @file{mcp.json}.  The project file
is @file{.harmless/mcp.json} in the session directory.  The global
file is @file{mcp.json} under the Harmless data directory.  A name
in both files uses the project command.  A detached chat reads only
the global file.  A relative directory, an empty directory, and the
filesystem root do not select a project file.

The file is a JSON object.  @code{mcpServers} maps a server name to
a @code{command} and an optional @code{args} list.  A name that is
not a single token is refused: @samp{MCP server name is not a
name}.  @code{sub} with surrounding spaces is that token.  A
missing command is refused: @samp{MCP command is empty}.  A command
that is not text is refused: @samp{MCP command must be a string}.
An argument that is not text is refused: @samp{MCP argument must
be a string}.  A file that is not JSON is refused: @samp{mcp.json
is not valid}.  A file with no @code{mcpServers} object is refused:
@samp{mcp.json has no servers}.  A missing file means no servers.

Each server tool is offered as @code{mcp__}, the server name,
@code{__}, and the tool name.  The offered schema is an empty
object.  Harmless loads the server's schema when the tool is about
to be called.  A missing required argument is refused:
@samp{MCP argument }, the name, and @samp{ is required}.  The class
is @code{network}.  A tool from the server is not offered to a
read-only subagent, because that tool can write.

@code{mcp_list_resources} lists resources for @code{server}.
@code{mcp_read_resource} reads @code{uri} from that server.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{server name is empty}
or @samp{resource uri is empty}.  A value that is not text is
refused: @samp{server name must be a string} or @samp{resource uri
must be a string}.  A server name that is not a token, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{server name is not a
name}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ordinary resource URIs.  An
unknown server is refused: @samp{no MCP server }, followed by the
name.  No resources returns @samp{No resources}.  A resource with
no text returns @samp{resource has no text}.

The tools ask in @code{ask} mode and in @code{accept-edits}.
@code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan mode does not refuse
them.  A read-only subagent may list and read resources.  A
detached chat may do that too.  A failed call is @samp{MCP call
failed}.  A command that is not a program is refused before it
starts: @samp{MCP command is not a program}.  Nothing is written
into the project.

@node Computer use
@section Computer use

@cindex computer use
@cindex computer_screenshot
@cindex computer_click
@cindex computer_type
@cindex computer_key
@cindex browser_open
Harmless can see the screen, click, type, and open a browser the
user can watch.

@code{computer_screenshot} returns the width, the height, and a PNG
path under the Harmless data directory, in @file{computer/}.  The
file is not in the project.

@code{computer_click} clicks at @code{x} and @code{y}.  A missing
@code{button} is left.  Nil and a value that is not an integer are
refused: @samp{x must be an integer} or @samp{y must be an
integer}.  A negative integer is refused: @samp{x must be a
non-negative integer}.  A button other than left, right, or middle
is refused: @samp{button must be left, right, or middle}.

@code{computer_type} types @code{text} into the focused window.
Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{text must be a
string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{text is
empty}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are characters to type.

@code{computer_key} presses one key, such as enter, tab, escape, a
function key from f1 to f12, or one character.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{key is empty}.  A value that is not
text is refused: @samp{key must be a string}.  Anything else,
including @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{key is not a key}.
@code{/} is a key.

@code{browser_open} opens @code{url} in the desktop browser.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{url is empty}.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{url must be a string}.
@code{/} and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{url must be http or
https}.

The class is @code{shell}.  The tools ask in @code{ask} mode and in
@code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  In plan
mode a click, typed text, a key, and the browser are refused before
the question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused}.
A screenshot still runs.  A read-only subagent is not offered
these tools.  A detached chat is offered them.  Nothing is written
into the project.

@node Monitors
@section Monitors

@cindex monitor
@cindex monitor_stop
@code{monitor} watches one source until a line matches
@code{pattern}.  The source is a @code{path} inside the project or
an added directory, a @code{command}, or the id of a background
@code{shell}.  Pass one of those.  None is refused: @samp{monitor
source is missing}.
More than one is refused: @samp{monitor source must be one}.

Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{pattern must
be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{pattern is empty}.  A regex Emacs cannot compile is refused:
@samp{pattern is not a pattern}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are
patterns, not paths.

A path is resolved inside the project or an added directory.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{path must be a string}.
@code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{Path is empty}.
@code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a path that leaves the project and every
added directory are refused: @samp{Path escapes project: }, followed
by the path.  A directory,
including a trailing slash, is refused: @samp{monitor path is a
directory}.  A missing file is refused: @samp{monitor file is
missing}.  Lines already in the file do not match.  Later lines
do.

A command uses the same read-only check as the shell.  A value
that is not text is refused: @samp{command must be a string}.
@code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{command is empty}.  A
shell id that is not text is refused: @samp{shell id
must be a string}.  A negative number is refused: @samp{shell id
is not an id}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{shell
id is empty}.  An unknown shell id is refused: @samp{no background
shell }, followed by the id.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ids, not
paths.

A match sends @samp{Monitor }, the id, @samp{ matched:}, and the
line.  The session is idle, so that starts a turn.  A session
that is already in a turn queues the line and keeps going.
@code{monitor_stop} stops the watch.  A command this monitor
started is killed.  A background shell that was only watched
keeps running.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused:
@samp{monitor id is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{monitor id must be a string}.  A negative number is refused:
@samp{monitor id is not an id}.  An unknown id is refused:
@samp{no monitor }, followed by the id.  Stopping a monitor that
has already stopped is refused: @samp{monitor }, the id, and
@samp{ is not running}.

A file watch does not ask.  A read-only command does not ask.  A
command that can write asks in @code{ask} mode and in
@code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  In plan
mode that command is refused: @samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell
command that can write is refused}.  A file watch and a read-only
command still run.  A read-only subagent may watch a file or a
read-only command.  A detached chat is not offered these tools.
Nothing is written into the project.  The watch is forgotten when
Emacs exits.

@node Schedules
@section Schedules

@cindex schedule
@cindex schedule_cancel
@code{schedule} sends @code{prompt} once, at a later time.  Pass
@code{delay} in seconds, or @code{at} as a UTC time such as
@code{2099-01-01T00:00:00Z}.  Pass one of those.  None is refused:
@samp{schedule time is missing}.  More than one is refused:
@samp{schedule time must be one}.

Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{prompt must
be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, @code{log.txt}, and
@code{sub/} are prompt text, not paths.  They are not resolved,
and they do not create a file or a directory.

A delay that is not a positive number is refused: @samp{delay
must be a positive number}.  Zero and a negative number are
refused the same way.  A time that is not text is refused:
@samp{at must be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{at is empty}.  A value Emacs cannot parse, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{at is not a time}.  A
time that has already passed is refused: @samp{schedule time has
passed}.

The prompt and the absolute time are saved with the session.
When that session is loaded, a schedule that is still in the
future is armed again.  A schedule that has expired is removed
and is not sent.  This is one prompt, once.  It does not repeat,
and it does not watch a queue or a context wall.

When the time arrives, the prompt is sent.  The session is idle,
so that starts a turn.  A session that is already in a turn
queues the prompt and keeps going.  @code{schedule_cancel}
removes a waiting prompt.  The prompt is not sent.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{schedule id is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{schedule id
must be a string}.  A negative number is refused: @samp{schedule
id is not an id}.  An unknown id is refused: @samp{no schedule },
followed by the id.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ids, not paths.

The class is @code{edit}.  The tools ask in @code{ask} mode, and
the question shows the prompt, or the id when cancelling.
@code{accept-edits} and @code{always-approve} do not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse scheduling.  The turn that runs later still
obeys plan mode.  A read-only subagent is not offered these
tools.  A workspace subagent may schedule.  A detached chat is
offered them.  Nothing is written into the project.  Forking a
session does not copy its schedules.

@node Todos
@section Todos

@cindex todo_write
@code{todo_write} keeps a list of work on the session.  Each item
has an id, content, a status, and the ids it depends on.  Status
is @code{pending}, @code{in_progress}, @code{completed}, or
@code{cancelled}.  A missing status on a new item is
@code{pending}.

@code{merge} true, or omitted, updates items by id and appends an
id that is new.  A field that is omitted stays as it was.
@code{merge} false replaces the whole list.  JSON null and
@code{[]} are an empty list.  A missing @code{todos} value is
refused: @samp{todos must be a list}.  A value that is not a list
is refused the same way.  A merge value that is not a boolean is
refused: @samp{todo merge must be a boolean}.

An item that is not an item is refused: @samp{todo must be an
item}.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{todo id
is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{todo id
must be a string}.  The same empty and non-text refusals apply
to content: @samp{todo content is empty} and @samp{todo content
must be a string}.  A new item needs content.  An update can
omit it.  A status outside the four names is refused:
@samp{todo status is not a status}.  An id repeated in one call
is refused: @samp{todo id is duplicate}.

@code{depends} lists ids.  Omitted, it stays as it was on an
update and is empty on a new item.  JSON null and @code{[]} clear
it.  A value that is not a list is refused: @samp{todo depends
must be a list}.  An entry that is not text is refused:
@samp{todo depends must be a string}.  An empty entry is refused:
@samp{todo depends is empty}.  A repeated entry is refused:
@samp{todo depends is duplicate}.  An item that depends on its
own id is refused: @samp{todo depends on itself}.  An unknown id
is refused: @samp{todo depends is missing}.  A cycle is refused:
@samp{todo depends is a cycle}.  A dependency does not block a
status change.  The list shows it.

@code{/}, @code{c:/}, @code{notes}, and @code{sub/} are text, not
paths.  They are not resolved, and they do not create a file or
a directory.  The list is saved with the session.  A fork copies
it.  A subagent starts with an empty list.  Loading a session
keeps the list.  A saved item that is not usable is dropped.  A
dependency that is missing, repeated, on itself, or that closes
a cycle is dropped.  The class is @code{read}, so the tool does
not ask.  Plan mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent
may update its own list.  A detached chat is offered the tool.
Nothing is written into the project.  The session header shows
how many items are pending or in progress.

@node Review
@section Review

@findex harmless-review
@findex review
@vindex harmless-review-function
@vindex harmless-review-max-bytes
@kindex R @r{(Harmless menu)}
@cindex review
@kbd{M-x harmless-review} reads the working tree, a base branch, or
one commit.  The menu key is @kbd{R}.  The model uses the same
choice through @code{review}.  Omit @code{base} and @code{commit}
to read the working tree.  @code{base} is a branch name, and the
diff is that branch compared with @code{HEAD}.  @code{commit} is
one commit, such as @code{HEAD} or a hash.  Passing both is
refused: @samp{review has both a base and a commit}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{review base is
empty} or @samp{review commit is empty}.  A value that is not
text is refused: @samp{review base must be a string} or
@samp{review commit must be a string}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, a
drive root, @code{~}, @code{..}, a leading dash, and any other
absolute path are refused and are not resolved: @samp{review base
is not a branch} or @samp{review commit is not a commit}.  A
name git does not know is refused the same way.  A range is not
one commit.

@code{findings} records the result.  Omit it and the tool returns
the diff, cut at @code{harmless-review-max-bytes} (100000).  A
cut diff ends with @samp{[truncated]}.  The stored review is left
unchanged.  JSON null and @code{[]} record an empty list, and
the result is @samp{No findings}.  A missing list is refused:
@samp{review findings must be a list}.  A value that is not a
list is refused the same way.  An entry that is not a finding is
refused: @samp{review finding must be an item}.

Each finding has a severity, text, and an optional file and line.
Severity is @code{high}, @code{medium}, or @code{low}.  Anything
else is refused: @samp{review severity is not a severity}.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{review finding is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{review
finding must be a string}.  A file that is present and empty is
refused: @samp{review file is empty}.  A file that is not text is
refused: @samp{review file must be a string}.  A line that is
present and is not a positive integer is refused: @samp{review
line is not a line}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, @code{notes}, and
@code{sub/} are text, not paths.  They are not resolved, and
they do not create a file or a directory.

The list replaces the previous review.  It is saved with the
session.  A fork copies it.  A subagent starts without one.
Loading a session keeps the review.  A saved review that is not
usable is dropped.  A saved finding that is not usable is
dropped.  The class is @code{read}, so the tool does not ask.
Plan mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may review
the project.  A detached chat is not offered the tool.  Git is
used only to read.  Nothing is written into the project, and a
finding is not applied as an edit.

A project that is not a git repository is refused: @samp{this
project is not a git repository}.  A missing directory is not
created.  Nil, @code{""}, a relative path, @code{~}, @code{/},
and a drive root are refused: @samp{This chat is not attached to
a project}.  They are not resolved.  When git cannot be found:
@samp{git is not available}.  When a diff cannot be read:
@samp{review could not read the diff}.

@code{harmless-review-function}, when set, is called by
@kbd{M-x harmless-review} as @code{(session args diff)}.  A list
of findings is recorded.  Nil leaves the stored review unchanged
and the command returns the diff.  The tool does not call it.
It must not start a turn, and it must not edit the project.
The session header shows how many findings are recorded.

@node Language server
@section Language server

@findex harmless-lsp
@findex lsp
@vindex harmless-lsp-query-function
@kindex L @r{(Harmless menu)}
@cindex lsp
@cindex language server
@code{lsp} asks a language server that is already running in Emacs.
The operation is @code{definition}, @code{references}, or
@code{diagnostics}.  @kbd{M-x harmless-lsp} asks the same way.  The
menu key is @kbd{L}.

@code{path} is a file in the project, or in a directory added for
this session.  A definition or a list of references also takes
@code{line} and @code{column}.  Both start at 1.  Diagnostics read
the whole file and take no position.  The file must already be open
in a buffer whose server is running.  Harmless does not start a
server, and it does not open the file to find one.

A definition or a reference is one line, @code{FILE:LINE:COLUMN}.
The column in that line starts at 1.  No location is @samp{No
definition} or @samp{No references}.  A diagnostic is
@code{LINE:COLUMN: SEVERITY: TEXT}.  Severity is @code{error},
@code{warning}, or @code{note}.  No diagnostic is @samp{No
diagnostics}.  Text that spans lines is folded onto one line.

The class is @code{read}, so the tool does not ask.  Plan mode does
not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may call it.  A detached chat
is not offered the tool.  Nothing is written into the project.
@code{write_file} still reports only the path and the byte count.
Diagnostics are a later @code{lsp} call.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace for the operation are refused:
@samp{lsp operation is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{lsp operation must be a string}.  Any other name is refused:
@samp{lsp operation is not an operation}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace for the path are refused:
@samp{lsp path is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{lsp path must be a string}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a drive
root are refused and are not expanded: @samp{lsp path is the
filesystem root}.  A missing file, a directory, and a trailing
slash are refused: @samp{lsp path is not a file}.  A path outside
the project and outside every added directory is refused:
@samp{Path escapes project: }, followed by the path.  A detached
chat with a relative path is refused: @samp{This chat is not
attached to a project}.

A missing line is refused: @samp{lsp line is empty}.  A missing
column is refused: @samp{lsp column is empty}.  Zero, a negative
number, and a value that is not an integer are refused:
@samp{lsp line is not a line} or @samp{lsp column is not a column}.
A position on diagnostics is refused: @samp{lsp diagnostics takes
no position}.

When no buffer is visiting the file, or that buffer has no server:
@samp{lsp server is not running}.  A failed request is refused:
@samp{lsp query failed}.

@code{harmless-lsp-query-function}, when set, is called instead of
the server as @code{(session op path line column)}.  @code{op} is
@code{definition}, @code{references}, or @code{diagnostics}.
@code{path} is absolute.  @code{line} and @code{column} are nil for
diagnostics.  It returns the text the model sees.  Nil is refused:
@samp{lsp query returned nothing}.  A value that is not text is
refused: @samp{lsp query must be a string}.  It must not start a
turn, and it must not edit the project.

@node Instructions
@chapter Instructions

@cindex project instructions
@cindex AGENTS.md
@cindex HARMLESS.md
@cindex CLAUDE.md
@vindex harmless-claude-md
Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

@enumerate
@item
@file{CLAUDE.md}, when @code{harmless-claude-md} says to read it

@item
@file{AGENTS.md}

@item
@file{HARMLESS.md}

@item
@file{.harmless/HARMLESS.md}
@end enumerate

@code{harmless-claude-md} has three values.  @code{nil} never reads
@file{CLAUDE.md}.  @code{t} always reads it.  @code{anthropic}, the
default, reads it only when the session model id starts with
@code{claude-}.  A model such as @code{grok-4.6} does not match.
@file{CLAUDE.md} is read before the other three names, so those later
files win when they disagree.

A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty
files are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

An instruction file can include another file by writing
@code{@@path}.  The path is relative to the file that contains the
import, not the session directory.  A backslash before a space is part
of the path.  The file's text replaces the import.  An import inside
inline code or a fenced code block is left as written.  A quoted path
is left as written.  The file must stay inside the session directory.
Harmless refuses an empty path, a missing file, a directory, a cycle,
and a path that leaves the session.  The filesystem root is not a
directory an import may enter.

The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk @code{default-directory}
looking for these files.

@heading Path-scoped rules
@cindex path-scoped rules
@cindex .claude/rules
Harmless also reads Markdown files under @file{.claude/rules/} in the
session directory.  Files in subdirectories are included.  The
subdirectory name does not choose which project files the rule applies
to.  A file with no @code{paths} field in its YAML frontmatter is sent
on every turn.  A file with @code{paths} is sent only when a tool call
in the transcript names a file that matches one of those globs.
@code{*} matches inside one path segment.  @code{**} matches across
segments, including none.  A rule in a parent directory is not read.
Nil, an empty string, a relative path, and the filesystem root are not
searched.

The rule text is added to the system message for that turn.  It is not
copied into the transcript.  When two rules disagree, the later file in
sorted path order wins.

@heading Standing prompt
@cindex harness prompt
Every turn starts with a standing harness prompt.  It states which
tool to use, when to verify, and when to delegate.  A project chat and
a detached chat both receive it.  Project instructions, path-scoped
rules, and, in plan mode, the plan rules are sent after it.  When they
disagree with the harness prompt, they win.  The prompt is not copied
into the transcript.  Nil, an empty string, and a relative directory
still receive the prompt, and they still do not walk
@code{default-directory}.

@node Skills
@chapter Skills

@cindex skills
@cindex SKILL.md
@findex harmless-load-skill
A skill is a directory that contains @file{SKILL.md}.  The file starts
with @code{name} and @code{description} in YAML frontmatter.  If the
name is missing, Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description
is missing, it uses the first paragraph of the body.

Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the path.
The body stays out of that catalog.  The model can still open the file
with @code{read_file}.

Load one skill with the @code{skill} tool, with
@kbd{M-x harmless-load-skill}, or by starting a prompt with
@code{/name}.  The name is a skill id, not a path.  Later turns
include the body.  The prompt that loaded it stays in the transcript.
A detached chat cannot load a skill, and it is not offered the tool.

@code{allowed-tools} in the frontmatter limits the tools on those
turns.  A missing field leaves the tools unchanged.  An empty list
offers no tools.  A tool name Harmless does not have is refused, and
the skill that was already loaded stays loaded.  A read-only subagent
still cannot write, even when the list names a writing tool.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{skill name is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{skill name must
be a string}.  A path, @code{~}, and the filesystem root are refused:
@samp{skill name is not a name}.  A missing skill is refused:
@samp{no skill named }, followed by the id.  A relative project
directory, including @code{~}, is refused and is not expanded:
@samp{skill directory must be absolute}.  @code{/} and a drive root
are refused: @samp{skill directory is the filesystem root}.

Harmless looks for a @file{skills} directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

@table @file
@item .agents/skills
@itemx .codex/skills
Codex.

@item .claude/skills
Claude Code.

@item .grok/skills
Grok Build.

@item .harmless/skills
Harmless.
@end table

In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier skill
of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of the
same name farther away.  @file{/etc/codex/skills} is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.

@node Plugins
@chapter Plugins

@cindex plugins
@cindex marketplace
@findex harmless-plugin-install
A plugin is one directory.  It can hold skills, agents, a
@file{hooks.json} file, and an @file{mcp.json} file.
@kbd{M-x harmless-plugin-install} copies that directory as one unit.
It does not merge those files into @file{.harmless/skills},
@file{.harmless/agents}, or the project's @file{mcp.json}.

The copy for a project is @file{.harmless/plugins/NAME}.  The copy
for the user is @file{plugins/NAME} under the Harmless data
directory.  A later plugin name wins when two installed plugins
define the same skill, agent, or MCP server.  A project copy wins
over a user copy.  Deleting the copied directory removes the bundle.

A marketplace is a directory.  When it contains
@file{marketplace.json}, that file is the list of plugins.
@code{source} is a relative path inside the marketplace.  An
absolute path, @code{~}, and a path that leaves the marketplace are
refused.  With no @file{marketplace.json}, each child directory
whose @file{plugin.json} name matches the directory is a plugin.

@file{plugin.json} names the plugin.  The name is a plugin id, not
a path.  Skills live in @file{skills/ID/SKILL.md}.  Agents live in
@file{agents/NAME.md}.  @file{hooks.json} is a list of hooks.
@file{mcp.json} uses the same @code{mcpServers} object as the
project file.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{plugin name is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{plugin name
must be a string}.  A path, @code{~}, and the filesystem root are
refused: @samp{plugin name is not a name}.  A missing plugin is
refused: @samp{no plugin named }, followed by the id.  A relative
marketplace, including @code{~}, is refused and is not expanded:
@samp{plugin directory must be absolute}.  @code{/} and a drive
root are refused: @samp{plugin directory is the filesystem root}.
A detached chat has no project copy: @samp{This chat is not
attached to a project}.  A second copy is refused: @samp{plugin is
already installed: }, followed by the id.

@node Output styles
@chapter Output styles

@cindex output style
@findex harmless-output-style
An output style is a role, tone, and format for one session.
@kbd{M-x harmless-output-style} switches it.  Switching does not
edit @file{AGENTS.md}, @file{HARMLESS.md}, @file{.harmless/HARMLESS.md},
or @file{CLAUDE.md}.

A style file is markdown.  Optional frontmatter holds @code{name}
and @code{description}.  The body is the text later turns send.
The name is a style id, not a path.  @code{default} is not a style.
Nil, @code{""}, and @code{default} clear the current style.
Whitespace is refused: @samp{output style name is empty}.  A value
that is not text is refused: @samp{output style name must be a
string}.  A path, @code{~}, and the filesystem root are refused:
@samp{output style name is not a name}.  A missing style is
refused: @samp{no output style named }, followed by the id.  An
empty body is refused: @samp{output style body is empty}.  The
previous style stays in place when a switch is refused.

User styles are @file{output-styles} under the Harmless data
directory, then @file{~/.claude/output-styles}.  Project styles are
@file{.claude/output-styles} and @file{.harmless/output-styles}
under the session directory only.  Ancestors are not scanned.  A
project style wins over a user style.  In the project,
@file{.harmless} wins over @file{.claude}.  A detached chat sees
user styles only.

A relative project directory, including @code{~}, is refused and
is not expanded: @samp{output style directory must be absolute}.
@code{/} and a drive root are refused: @samp{output style directory
is the filesystem root}.  A blank project directory is refused:
@samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.

The chosen name and body are saved with the session.  Loading the
session restores them.  A fork keeps the style it was given.  The
harness prompt stays first.  The style comes next.  Project
instructions, path-scoped rules, and plan mode win when they
disagree.

@node Image generation
@chapter Image generation

@cindex image generation
@findex generate_image
@code{generate_image} writes an image into the project.  The model
passes @code{prompt} and @code{path}.  A relative path is resolved
against the session directory.  It is not resolved against the
buffer's default directory.  @code{~} is not expanded.

The bytes come from @code{harmless-image-generate-function}.  When
that variable is nil, the tool is refused: @samp{image generation
is not configured}.  The function is not called until the prompt
and the path are accepted.  Plan mode does not call it:
@samp{Plan mode is on. Only plan.md may be edited, with write_plan}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{image prompt is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{image prompt
must be a string}.  A missing or blank path is refused:
@samp{image path is empty}.  A path that is not text is refused:
@samp{image path must be a string}.  A path that names a directory
is refused: @samp{image path is not a file}.  @code{~} is refused:
@samp{image path must be absolute}.  A path outside the project is
refused: @samp{image path escapes the project: }, followed by the
path.  Bytes that are not an image are refused: @samp{image is not
an image}.  Nothing is written in that case.

A detached chat and a blank project directory are refused:
@samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.  A relative project
directory is refused and is not expanded: @samp{image directory
must be absolute}.  @code{/} and a drive root are refused:
@samp{image directory is the filesystem root}.  @code{/} as a path
is refused: @samp{image path is the filesystem root}.

The tool is an edit.  A detached chat is not offered it.  A
successful call reports the absolute path and the number of bytes
written.

@node Headless
@chapter Headless

@cindex headless
@findex harmless-task-start
@findex harmless-task-status
@findex harmless-headless-batch
@findex harmless-headless-work
A headless run sends one prompt and saves the session.  It does not
open a window.  A task starts another Emacs on this machine to run
that prompt.  Closing the Emacs that started the task does not stop
the other process.  The task file is what a later Emacs reads.

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-task-start
Ask for a project directory and a prompt.  Write a task file under
@code{harmless-directory} and start the other Emacs.  The command
returns the task id.  It does not wait for the reply.

@item M-x harmless-task-status
Read that task file.  The status is @code{running}, @code{done}, or
@code{error}.

@item emacs --batch -l harmless -f harmless-headless-batch
Run one prompt in this process and print the reply.  The next two
arguments are the project directory and the prompt.  This exits
Emacs.

@item emacs --batch -l harmless -f harmless-headless-work
Finish the task id given as the next argument.  This is the entry
the other Emacs runs.  This exits Emacs.
@end table

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{headless prompt is
empty}.  A prompt that is not text is refused: @samp{headless prompt
must be a string}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{headless directory
is empty}.  A directory that is not text is refused: @samp{headless
directory must be a string}.  A relative path, including @code{~},
is refused and is not expanded: @samp{headless directory must be
absolute}.  @code{/} and a drive root are refused: @samp{headless
directory is the filesystem root}.  A path that is not there is
refused: @samp{headless directory is missing}.  A file is refused:
@samp{headless directory is not a directory}.  A trailing slash is
the same directory.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{headless task is
empty}.  A task id that is not text is refused: @samp{headless task
must be a string}.  A path is refused: @samp{headless task is not a
name}.  An unknown id is refused: @samp{headless task is missing}.
A task that is not running is refused: @samp{headless task is not
running}.  A broken task file is refused: @samp{headless task is not
valid}.

A task file is refused at the filesystem root: @samp{Refusing to
store a headless task at }, followed by that directory.  A turn that
does not finish with a reply is refused: @samp{headless turn
failed}.  The task file then says @code{error} and keeps that text.

A finished task stores the reply and the session id.  The transcript
is the session file a window would have saved.  In batch Emacs, a
tool that would ask is denied.  The turn still ends.

@node Pages
@chapter Pages

@cindex pages
@findex harmless-publish
@kbd{M-x harmless-publish} writes one HTML page from a Markdown or
HTML file in the project.  The source file is not modified.

Nil PATH writes @file{.harmless/pages/NAME.html}, where NAME is the
source file's name without its extension.  A relative destination
stays inside the project.  An absolute destination is written only
when its parent already exists.  @code{~} is not expanded.

Markdown becomes a document.  A heading line is @code{#} through
@code{######} followed by a space.  A blank line separates
paragraphs.  A fenced block is three backticks.  Inline code is one
backtick span.  A link is @code{[label](target)}.  Those parts are
rendered.  The other text is escaped, so a @code{<script>} in the
Markdown is text.

An HTML file that already contains a document is copied.  A
fragment is wrapped in a document.  Publishing again overwrites
the page.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{page directory
is empty}.  A directory that is not text is refused: @samp{page
directory must be a string}.  A relative project directory,
including @code{~}, is refused and is not expanded: @samp{page
directory must be absolute}.  @code{/} and a drive root are
refused: @samp{page directory is the filesystem root}.  A missing
project directory is refused: @samp{page directory is missing}.  A
file is refused: @samp{page directory is not a directory}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{page source is
empty}.  A source that is not text is refused: @samp{page source
must be a string}.  @code{/} and a drive root are refused:
@samp{page source is the filesystem root}.  @code{~} is refused
and is not expanded: @samp{page source must be absolute}.  A
relative source that leaves the project is refused: @samp{page
source escapes the project: }, followed by the path.  A missing
source is refused: @samp{page source is missing}.  A directory is
refused: @samp{page source is not a file}.  A file that is not
Markdown or HTML is refused: @samp{page source is not a page}.

Nil is the default destination.  @code{""} and whitespace are
refused: @samp{page path is empty}.  A path that is not text is
refused: @samp{page path must be a string}.  @code{/} and a drive
root are refused: @samp{page path is the filesystem root}.
@code{~} is refused: @samp{page path must be absolute}.  A
trailing slash is refused: @samp{page path is not a file}.  A
relative path that leaves the project is refused: @samp{page path
escapes the project: }, followed by the path.  A destination that
is not @file{.html} or @file{.htm} is refused: @samp{page path is
not a page}.  An absolute destination whose parent is missing is
refused: @samp{page path is missing}.  The source file as the
destination is refused: @samp{page path is the source}.

@node Team
@chapter Team

@cindex team
@findex harmless-team-add-seat
@findex harmless-team-occupy
@findex harmless-team-add-work
@findex harmless-team-seat
@findex harmless-team-edge
@findex harmless-team-edges
@findex harmless-team-launch-order
@findex harmless-team-preview
@findex harmless-team-boot
@findex harmless-team-grow
@findex harmless-team-remove
@findex harmless-team-adopt
@findex harmless-team-release
@findex harmless-team-send
@findex harmless-team-capture
@findex harmless-team-transcript
@findex harmless-team-room-say
@findex harmless-team-room-history
@findex harmless-team-room-wait
@findex harmless-team-pace
@findex harmless-team-pace-add
@findex harmless-team-pace-show
@findex harmless-team-pace-step
@findex harmless-team-row
@findex harmless-team-rows
@findex harmless-team-row-read
@findex harmless-team-row-claim
@findex harmless-team-row-note
@findex harmless-team-row-close
@findex harmless-team-row-hand
@findex harmless-team-row-park
@findex harmless-team-row-resolve
@findex harmless-team-intake
@findex harmless-team-intakes
@findex harmless-team-board
@findex harmless-team-workflow
@findex harmless-team-workflows
@findex harmless-team-watchdog
@findex harmless-team-watchdogs
@findex harmless-team-watchdog-check
@findex harmless-team-snapshot
@findex harmless-team-snapshot-check
@findex harmless-team-snapshot-restore
@findex harmless-team-successor
@findex harmless-team-boundaries
@findex harmless-team-image
@findex harmless-team-image-start
@findex harmless-team-packet
@findex harmless-team-packet-apply
@findex harmless-team-compact-plan
@findex harmless-team-compact
@findex harmless-team-compacts
A rig is a named team.  A pod is a named group of seats.  A seat
has a name, a role, and an address.  The address is
@code{pod-member@@rig}, for example @code{dev-build@@workshop}.
The session that occupies the seat can be replaced.  The seat
keeps its name, its role, and its inbound work.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-add-seat} records the seat.  It starts
with no session and no work.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-occupy}
sets the session id.  Calling it again replaces that session.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-add-work} appends one text to the inbound
work.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-seat} reads the seat back.

The record is @file{teams/@var{rig}.json} under
@code{harmless-directory}.  @xref{Files}.  A pod name and a seat
name are letters and digits.  A rig name may also contain
hyphens.  The address uses the stored spelling.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-edge} records one edge.  The ends are
@code{pod.member} or the address.  @code{delegates_to} starts
the source before the target.  @code{spawned_by} starts the
target before the source.  @code{can_observe},
@code{collaborates_with}, and @code{escalates_to} are recorded
and shown, and they do not change the order.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-edges} reads the edges.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-launch-order} reads the order.  It does
not start a seat.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-preview} shows the seats, the edges, and
the launch order a boot would record.  It does not write.  The
spec is the name @code{starter}, an absolute JSON file, or a
directory that contains @file{rig.json}.  @code{starter} records
two seats and one @code{delegates_to} edge.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-boot} records that rig.  Booting the same
rig again is refused: @samp{team rig already exists}.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-grow} adds a seat to a rig that is already
recorded.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-remove} shrinks the rig: it
drops a seat and every edge that touches it.  A seat that still
holds work needs another seat to take that work.  Without one
the removal is refused: @samp{team seat holds work}.  Naming the
same seat is refused: @samp{team fallback is the same seat}.
The rig file stays when its last seat is removed.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-adopt} binds a session that is already
running to a seat.  It does not start that session.  The runtime
is @code{harmless}, @code{claude}, @code{codex}, @code{pi}, or
@code{terminal}.  A Harmless session is the id of a session
directory that already exists, including one started by hand.
Claude Code, Codex, and Pi each take an absolute path to a file
or directory that already exists.  A terminal takes that path,
or the id of a live process such as a dev server or a log.
Replacing the binding does not stop the session that was there.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-release} clears the binding.  It does not
delete the session, the file, or the process.  The name, the
role, and the inbound work stay.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-send} types text onto one seat, a
comma-separated list of seats, a pod, the rig name, or
@code{*}.  The text is appended to that seat's screen, and the
send is refused when the text is not there afterward.  It does
not start a turn.  A Harmless seat, including one filled by
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-occupy}, gets one user line in
@file{messages.jsonl}.  A terminal log is the screen and is
appended.  Claude Code, Codex, and Pi use @file{screen.txt} in
the session directory.  A live process id is not typed into:
@samp{team seat has no screen}.  A directory with no
@file{screen.txt} is refused the same way.

The whole send is refused before any screen changes when a
seat is empty (@samp{team seat is not occupied}), at a prompt
(@samp{team seat is at a prompt}), or at a permission question
(@samp{team seat is at a permission question}).  A prompt is a
last line @code{>} or a last line that ends in @code{>}.
@code{Allow this tool?} is a permission question.  A Harmless
seat uses its status instead of the message file.
@code{streaming} is a prompt and @code{waiting-permission} is a
permission question.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-capture} reads the screen.  A process id
has no file, so the capture is the transcript.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-transcript} reads every text sent to that
seat, including text sent to an earlier occupant.  Releasing
the seat, or removing it, does not delete that history.

A blank send is refused: @samp{team send is empty}.  A send
that is not text is refused: @samp{team send must be a string}.
A target that names nothing is refused: @samp{team send is not
a target}.  A missing seat is refused: @samp{team seat is
missing}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-room-say} appends one line to the rig's
chatroom.  The line records the topic, the speaker, the text,
and the time.  A topic is letters and digits.  The speaker is
any text.  The room is kept in the rig file.  It does not start
a turn.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-room-history} reads the lines,
oldest first.  With no topic, it reads every topic.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-room-wait} blocks until someone speaks
in that topic.  Lines already stored do not count.  The wait
stops when nobody has spoken within
@code{harmless-team-room-wait-seconds} (10 seconds):
@samp{team room is still quiet}.

A blank topic is refused: @samp{team room topic is empty}.  A
topic that is not text is refused: @samp{team room topic must
be a string}.  Any other topic is refused: @samp{team room
topic is not a name}.  A speaker uses the empty and
must-be-a-string sentences, with @code{speaker} in place of
@code{topic}.  The text uses them too, with @code{text}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-pace} starts a named sequence aimed at
one seat.  The seat is @code{pod.member} or the address.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-pace-add} appends one piece.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-pace-show} reads the sequence and does
not send.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-pace-step} sends the next
piece to that seat, then moves forward.  One call sends one
piece.  There is no command that sends the rest at once.  A
seat at a prompt or a permission question refuses the step,
and the sequence stays on that piece.  A finished sequence is
refused: @samp{team pace is finished}.

A blank pace name is refused: @samp{team pace is empty}.  A
name that is not text is refused: @samp{team pace must be a
string}.  Any other name is refused: @samp{team pace is not a
name}.  Piece text uses the empty and must-be-a-string
sentences, with @code{text} in place of @code{pace}.  The same
name again is refused: @samp{team pace already exists}.  A
missing pace is refused: @samp{team pace is missing}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row} opens one unit of owned work aimed
at one seat.  The seat is @code{pod.member} or the address.
The body is the work.  The state is @code{open}.  The history
starts with one line, @code{opened}.  The row is kept in the
rig file, so it stays when the seat's session is replaced,
released, or removed.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-rows} reads the
rows, oldest first.  With no seat, it reads every row.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-read} reads one row by its id.
Reading by id does not need the destination seat to still
exist.

A blank body is refused: @samp{team row body is empty}.  A
body that is not text is refused: @samp{team row body must be
a string}.  A blank id is refused: @samp{team row is empty}.
An id that is not text is refused: @samp{team row must be a
string}.  Any other id is refused: @samp{team row is not an
id}.  A missing row is refused: @samp{team row is missing}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-claim} holds an open row.  The
history gains one line, @code{claimed}.  A held row is refused:
@samp{team row is held}.  A closed row is refused: @samp{team
row is closed}.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-note} appends one
note to an open or held row.  A closed row is refused:
@samp{team row is closed}.  A blank note is refused:
@samp{team row note is empty}.  A note that is not text is
refused: @samp{team row note must be a string}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-close} closes a held row.  An open
row is refused: @samp{team row is not held}.  The reason is
@code{handed_off_to}, @code{blocked_on}, @code{denied},
@code{canceled}, @code{no-follow-on}, or @code{escalation}.
The history gains one line, @code{delivered}, and the reason
is stored.  Closing records delivery.  Acceptance stays the
next seat's verdict, and this command does not open another
row.  A blank reason is refused: @samp{team row reason is
empty}.  A reason that is not text is refused: @samp{team row
reason must be a string}.  Any other reason is refused:
@samp{team row reason is not a reason}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-hand} hands a held row to another
seat.  The source closes with reason @code{handed_off_to} and
the destination opens with the same body.  Both changes are
written together.  The destination stays open, so acceptance
remains that seat's own later claim.  An open source is
refused: @samp{team row is not held}.  A closed source is
refused: @samp{team row is closed}.  The source's own seat is
refused: @samp{team row hand is the same seat}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-park} parks a held row on one wake.
A second wake is refused: @samp{team row is parked}.  An open
row is refused: @samp{team row is not held}.  A blank wake is
refused: @samp{team row wake is empty}.  A wake that is not
text is refused: @samp{team row wake must be a string}.  A
summary and a pointer to the evidence together ask a person.
The summary is plain language.  The evidence is text, such as
a path or a note, and it is stored as text.  One without the
other is refused: @samp{team row evidence is empty} or
@samp{team row summary is empty}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-row-resolve} records the decision in the
history and returns the row to held.  That is the owner's wake.
It does not start a turn.  A row that is not parked is refused:
@samp{team row is not parked}.  A blank decision is refused:
@samp{team row decision is empty}.  A decision that is not text
is refused: @samp{team row decision must be a string}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-intake} records an observation on the
rig.  It has no seat and no owner.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-intakes}
reads them, oldest first.  A blank observation is refused:
@samp{team intake is empty}.  An observation that is not text
is refused: @samp{team intake must be a string}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-board} reads one view of the rows.
@code{held} is a row in @code{held} or @code{parked}.
@code{stalled} is a held row with nothing after its claim.
@code{waiting} is a parked row that asks a person.  The view
does not write.  A blank view is refused: @samp{team board is
empty}.  A view that is not text is refused: @samp{team board
must be a string}.  Any other view is refused: @samp{team board
is not a view}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-workflow} declares an ordered list of
steps and opens the first one.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-workflows}
reads them, oldest first.  Closing the current step opens the
next step's row in the same write.  The owner decides that the
step is done.  The reason records delivery of this step.  The
next row stays open.  The last close opens no row.  Handing the
current step is refused: @samp{team row is a workflow step}.
A blank name is refused: @samp{team workflow is empty}.  A name
that is not text is refused: @samp{team workflow must be a
string}.  Any other name is refused: @samp{team workflow is not
a name}.  No steps is refused: @samp{team workflow has no
steps}.  Steps that are not a list are refused: @samp{team
workflow steps must be a list}.  A step without a seat and a
body is refused: @samp{team workflow step is not a step}.  A
blank body is refused: @samp{team workflow body is empty}.  A
body that is not text is refused: @samp{team workflow body must
be a string}.  A workflow that already exists is refused:
@samp{team workflow already exists}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-watchdog} records one job on the rig:
a reminder, a pool of artifacts, a nudge to a workflow, an
idle queue, or a seat near its context wall.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-watchdogs} reads them, oldest first.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-watchdog-check} reads the jobs that are
due.  The job stays in the rig file, so it is still there
after Emacs restarts.  The check does not arm a timer, a
schedule, or a monitor, and it does not start a turn.  A
reminder is due when its time has arrived.  An artifact pool
is stored text and is not opened.  A nudge is due while that
workflow still has a row.  An idle job is due while its seat
has an open row.  A wall is due when the seat's last prompt
reaches the recorded percent of the known context window.  A
blank kind is refused: @samp{team watchdog is empty}.  A kind
that is not text is refused: @samp{team watchdog must be a
string}.  Any other kind is refused: @samp{team watchdog is
not a kind}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-snapshot} stores a named copy of the rig
in its own file.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-snapshot-check} reads
that copy and reports each seat: @code{resumed}, @code{rebuilt},
@code{fresh}, @code{waiting}, or @code{failed}.  The check does
not write.  A fresh start is a list of seats, or one seat, and
the report labels it @code{fresh}.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-snapshot-restore} writes that copy back
only when no seat failed.  A failed seat is refused:
@samp{team snapshot cannot restore}.  The rig file is left
unchanged.  A blank name is refused: @samp{team snapshot is
empty}.  A name that is not text is refused: @samp{team snapshot
must be a string}.  Any other name is refused: @samp{team
snapshot is not a name}.  A snapshot that already exists is
refused: @samp{team snapshot already exists}.  A missing
snapshot is refused: @samp{team snapshot is missing}.  A fresh
value that is not a list is refused: @samp{team snapshot fresh
must be a list}.  A seat that is not in the snapshot is
refused: @samp{team seat is missing}.  A snapshot file that
cannot be read is refused: @samp{team snapshot is not valid}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-successor} hands one seat to a session
that is already running.  The seat keeps its name, its edges,
and its inbound work.  The recap of decisions is written at
that boundary.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-boundaries} reads those
recaps, oldest first.  The earlier session keeps running.  A
blank recap is refused: @samp{team recap is empty}.  A recap
that is not text is refused: @samp{team recap must be a
string}.  An empty seat is refused: @samp{team seat is not
occupied}.  The same session again is refused: @samp{team
successor is the same session}.  Nothing is written when the
successor cannot be bound.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-image} captures an occupied seat into
its own file.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-image-start} records
another seat from that file.  The new seat copies the role and
the inbound work, and it starts with no session.  The source
seat stays.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-packet} captures what that
seat knows: the recap, or the inbound work when there is no
recap.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-packet-apply} puts that work
back on the same seat and binds a session that is already
running.  The runtime must change.  The seat keeps its name.
Nothing is started.  A blank image name is refused:
@samp{team image is empty}.  A name that is not text is
refused: @samp{team image must be a string}.  Any other name
is refused: @samp{team image is not a name}.  An image that
already exists is refused: @samp{team image already exists}.
A missing image is refused: @samp{team image is missing}.  An
image file that cannot be read is refused: @samp{team image
is not valid}.  An empty seat is refused: @samp{team seat is
not occupied}.  A seat that is already recorded is refused:
@samp{team seat already exists}.  A blank packet name is
refused: @samp{team packet is empty}.  A name that is not text
is refused: @samp{team packet must be a string}.  Any other
name is refused: @samp{team packet is not a name}.  A packet
that already exists is refused: @samp{team packet already
exists}.  A missing packet is refused: @samp{team packet is
missing}.  A packet file that cannot be read is refused:
@samp{team packet is not valid}.  A seat that knows nothing is
refused: @samp{team packet is empty}.  The same runtime is
refused: @samp{team packet is the same runtime}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-team-compact-plan} lists Harmless seats at or
above a percent of the context window.  It does not write, and
it does not compact.  @kbd{M-x harmless-team-compact} records
why that seat is compacted and tells the room, on the topic
@code{compact}.  The seat must already be at that wall.
@kbd{M-x harmless-team-compacts} reads those notices, oldest
first.  The transcript summary is @kbd{M-x harmless-compact}.
Nothing is started.  A blank percent is refused: @samp{team
compact percent is empty}.  Any other percent is refused:
@samp{team compact percent is not a percent}.  A blank reason
is refused: @samp{team compact why is empty}.  A reason that is
not text is refused: @samp{team compact why must be a string}.
An empty seat is refused: @samp{team seat is not occupied}.  A
seat below the wall is refused: @samp{team seat is not near
the wall}.

A blank runtime is refused: @samp{team runtime is empty}.  A
runtime that is not text is refused: @samp{team runtime must be
a string}.  Any other runtime is refused: @samp{team runtime is
not a runtime}.  A blank session is refused: @samp{team session
is empty}.  A session that is not text is refused: @samp{team
session must be a string}.  A Harmless id that is not a session
id is refused: @samp{team session is not an id}.  A missing
session is refused: @samp{team session is missing}.  Two
Harmless directories with the same id are refused: @samp{team
session is not one session}.  @code{/} and a drive root are
refused: @samp{team session is the filesystem root}.  @code{~}
and a relative path are refused and are not expanded:
@samp{team session must be absolute}.  A terminal process that
is not running is refused: @samp{team session is not running}.
Releasing an empty seat is refused: @samp{team seat is not
occupied}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{team edge is
empty}.  An edge that is not text is refused: @samp{team edge
must be a string}.  Any other kind is refused: @samp{team edge
is not a kind}.  An end that is not a seat reference is
refused: @samp{team edge is not a seat}.  An edge from a seat
to itself is refused: @samp{team edge is the same seat}.  The
same edge again is refused: @samp{team edge already exists}.
An edge that would loop the launch order is refused:
@samp{team edge is a cycle}.  The rig file is left unchanged.

A blank spec is refused: @samp{team spec is empty}.  A spec that
is not text is refused: @samp{team spec must be a string}.
@code{/} and a drive root are refused: @samp{team spec is the
filesystem root}.  @code{~} and a relative path are refused and
are not expanded: @samp{team spec must be absolute}.  A missing
file, or a directory with no @file{rig.json}, is refused:
@samp{team spec is missing}.  A broken spec is refused:
@samp{team spec is not valid}.  The spec file is left unchanged.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{team rig is
empty}.  A rig that is not text is refused: @samp{team rig must
be a string}.  A path is refused: @samp{team rig is not a name}.
A pod and a seat use the same three sentences, with @code{pod}
or @code{seat} in place of @code{rig}.  A role uses the empty
and must-be-a-string sentences.  A session uses them too, and an
id that is not a session id is refused: @samp{team session is
not an id}.  Work text uses the empty and must-be-a-string
sentences.

A second seat with the same name is refused: @samp{team seat
already exists}.  A missing rig is refused: @samp{team rig is
missing}.  A missing seat is refused: @samp{team seat is
missing}.  A broken rig file is refused: @samp{team rig is not
valid}.  That file is left unchanged.

A team file is refused at the filesystem root: @samp{Refusing to
store a team at }, followed by that directory.

@node Scope
@chapter Scope

@cindex scope
@findex harmless-scope-project
@findex harmless-scope-mission
@findex harmless-scope-slice
@findex harmless-scope-show
@findex harmless-scope-proof
@findex harmless-scope-judge
@findex harmless-scope-progress
A project directory holds missions, and a mission holds slices.
@kbd{M-x harmless-scope-project} writes @file{SPEC.md} in the
project.  @kbd{M-x harmless-scope-mission} writes
@file{missions/NAME/SPEC.md}.  @kbd{M-x harmless-scope-slice}
writes @file{missions/NAME/slices/NAME/SPEC.md}.  Each file
starts with @code{# Intent}.  Only a slice also has
@code{# Specification}.  @kbd{M-x harmless-scope-show} composes
the project intent, the mission intent, and the slice intent.
Show writes nothing.

The specification is one item per non-blank line.
@kbd{M-x harmless-scope-proof} stores evidence as
@file{proof/NAME.md} under the slice.
@kbd{M-x harmless-scope-judge} appends one attributed
@code{accept}, @code{reject}, or @code{withdraw} to
@file{judgments.json}.  The judgment names the judge, the
reason, and the proof name.  @kbd{M-x harmless-scope-progress}
derives @code{proved}, @code{rejected}, or @code{open} from the
latest judgment whose proof file is still in the slice.  The
slice is @code{proved} only when it has an item and each latest
verdict is @code{accept}.  One latest @code{reject} is
@code{rejected}.  Anything else, including @code{withdraw}, is
@code{open}.
Progress writes nothing.  Nothing is started.

A name is letters and digits.  @code{/} is valid intent,
specification, proof text, judge, and reason, and it is not
opened as a path.  A relative project, including @code{~}, is
refused and is not expanded.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{scope project
is empty}.  A project that is not text is refused:
@samp{scope project must be a string}.  @code{/} and a drive
root are refused: @samp{scope project is the filesystem root}.
A relative project is refused: @samp{scope project must be
absolute}.  A missing project is refused: @samp{scope project
is missing}.  A file is refused: @samp{scope project is not a
directory}.  A second project intent is refused: @samp{scope
project already exists}.  The project directory is not created.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{scope intent
is empty}.  Intent that is not text is refused: @samp{scope
intent must be a string}.  A specification uses the same two
sentences, with @code{spec} in place of @code{intent}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{scope mission
is empty}.  A mission that is not text is refused:
@samp{scope mission must be a string}.  Any other mission is
refused: @samp{scope mission is not a name}.  A missing mission
is refused: @samp{scope mission is missing}.  A second mission
is refused: @samp{scope mission already exists}.  A project
with no intent is refused: @samp{scope project has no intent}.
A slice, a proof, and an evidence name use the same three
sentences, with @code{slice}, @code{proof}, or @code{evidence}
in place of @code{mission}.  A missing slice is refused:
@samp{scope slice is missing}.  A second slice is refused:
@samp{scope slice already exists}.  A second proof is refused:
@samp{scope proof already exists}.  A missing proof is refused:
@samp{scope evidence is missing}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{scope item is
empty}.  Zero, a past-the-end number, and any other item are
refused:
@samp{scope item is not an item}.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{scope verdict is empty}.  A
verdict that is not text is refused: @samp{scope verdict must
be a string}.  Any other verdict is refused: @samp{scope
verdict is not a verdict}.  A judge and a reason use the empty
and must-be-a-string sentences, with @code{judge} or
@code{reason} in place of @code{verdict}.

A slice with no specification is refused: @samp{scope slice is
not specified}.  A file that is not intent is refused:
@samp{scope file is not valid}.  A judgment file that cannot be
read is refused: @samp{scope judgments are not valid}.  That
file is left unchanged.  A scope path at the filesystem root is
refused: @samp{Refusing to store scope at }, followed by that
path.

@node Memory
@chapter Memory

@findex harmless-remember
@findex harmless-dream
@cindex memory
Memory is Markdown under @code{harmless-directory}, in @file{memory}.
Global notes apply to every project.  Workspace notes belong to one
project.  A detached chat has workspace memory unavailable and global
memory available.

Each scope has three directories:

@table @file
@item topics
Curated notes, one file per topic.

@item observations/_inbox
New facts, not yet folded into a topic.

@item archive
Inbox files that @code{harmless-dream} has folded.
@end table

@file{MEMORY.md} in each scope is a generated index.  A turn receives
the index, not the notes.

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-remember
Save a title and a note in workspace memory.  A prefix argument saves
it in global memory.

@item M-x harmless-dream
Fold the inbox into topics for this project and for global memory.
The inbox files move to @file{archive}.
@end table

The model uses three tools.

@table @code
@item memory_read
Read one memory file.  @code{scope} is @code{workspace} or
@code{global}.

@item memory_remember
Append a fact to the inbox.  @code{topic} is an optional slug, such as
@code{emacs}.

@item memory_write_topic
Replace one topic file with the full Markdown you supply.
@end table

A finished turn can write one inbox observation.  Set
@code{harmless-memory-observe-function} to a function of the session.
It returns a plist with @code{:title} and @code{:body}, and optional
@code{:topic} and @code{:scope}.  Nil writes nothing.  The function
is not called when the turn aborts or the reply is empty.  A tool
call does not write one until the turn is idle.  The transcript is
not modified.  An unset function writes nothing.

A detached chat records a global observation.  An attached project
records a workspace observation.  @code{scope} may name
@code{workspace} or @code{global}.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace for the title or the body write
nothing.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{memory title
must be a string}, @samp{memory body must be a string}, or
@samp{memory topic must be a string}.  A scope that is not a scope
is refused: @samp{memory scope is not a scope}.  A scope that is
not text is refused: @samp{memory scope must be a string}.  A
relative project directory, including @code{~}, is refused and is
not expanded: @samp{memory directory must be absolute}.  @code{/}
and a drive root are refused: @samp{memory directory is the
filesystem root}.  A blank project directory is refused:
@samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.  Not a session is
refused: @samp{No Harmless session}.

@node Plan mode
@chapter Plan mode

@findex harmless-plan
@findex enter_plan_mode
@findex write_plan
@findex exit_plan_mode
@cindex plan mode
@kbd{M-x harmless-plan} turns plan mode on for the current session.  A
prefix argument turns it off.  The menu key is @kbd{P}.  The model may
also call @code{enter_plan_mode}, which asks you before it switches.

While plan mode is on, the model is told to explore with read-only
tools, to write the plan only with @code{write_plan}, and to call
@code{exit_plan_mode} when the plan is ready.  @code{write_file},
@code{replace}, @code{apply_patch}, @code{notebook_edit}, and
@code{generate_image} fail.  A
shell command that can write is refused before any permission
question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can write is refused}.
A read-only shell command still runs.  @code{shell_kill} still asks.
A click, typed text, a key, and a browser are refused:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused}.
A screenshot still runs.  @xref{Computer use}.
A file watch and a read-only command still run.  A command that
can write is refused.  @xref{Monitors}.
Scheduling a prompt still runs.  The later turn obeys plan mode.
@xref{Schedules}.
A review still runs, and it does not edit the project.
@xref{Review}.
A language server query still runs, and it does not edit the
project.  @xref{Language server}.
The header line shows @samp{plan}.
@code{ask_user} can still ask a question.  @xref{Questions}.

@code{write_plan} writes @file{plan.md} in the session directory.  The
contents replace the whole file.  An empty plan is refused.

@code{exit_plan_mode} shows the plan and asks:

@example
Plan: a approve and build, c comment, s request changes, q quit
@end example

@table @kbd
@item a
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to implement the plan.

@item c
Comment on the line at point.  An active region comments on that
range of lines.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused:
@samp{plan comment is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{plan comment must be a string}.  A missing line, a line past
the end, and a value that is not a positive integer are refused:
@samp{plan comment line is not a line}.  A range whose end comes
before its start is refused: @samp{plan comment range is
backwards}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are comments, not paths.  A
comment does not change @file{plan.md}.

@item s
Ask for revision notes, leave plan mode on, and send those notes
back.  Comments already entered are included.  One line is sent as
@samp{Line N: TEXT}.  A range is sent as @samp{Lines N-M: TEXT}.

@item q
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to stop without changing project
files.
@end table

Approving does not apply a patch by itself.  The same turn continues,
and later tool calls do the edits.

A read-only shell command still runs.  A command that can write is
refused, including when the permission mode is @code{always-approve}.
@xref{Shell}.

The flag is saved with the session.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@findex harmless-usage
@vindex harmless-usage-context-windows
@kindex g @r{(usage)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-usage} shows two blocks.

The session block is the context window of the current model, the
prompt size of the last request, and what remains in the window.  It
then shows the running prompt and completion totals for this chat.
Window sizes that Harmless does not learn from the provider live in
@code{harmless-usage-context-windows}.  A model with no known window
is marked unknown.  Before the next prompt, Harmless compacts the
transcript when that prompt size has reached
@code{harmless-compact-threshold} percent of the window.
@xref{Transcript}.

The account block is the last rate-limit remainder each provider sent.
A connection with no such headers says so.  For the connection named
@samp{xAI}, the buffer also asks xAI for the current allowance when
you are not in batch Emacs.  This report is not an account credit
balance.

In the usage buffer, @kbd{g} redraws and @kbd{q} quits.

@node Files
@chapter Files

@vindex harmless-directory
@cindex session files
Harmless keeps its own files under @code{harmless-directory}.  The
default is @file{harmless/} in @code{user-emacs-directory}.  Nil and
the empty string mean that same default.  A relative path is refused.
The filesystem root is refused.  A missing directory is created when
it is needed.  It is not replaced with @file{/}.

@table @file
@item config.el
Optional configuration, loaded once.

@item auth.json
@itemx auth-openai.json
@itemx auth-anthropic.json
@itemx auth-@var{slug}.json
OAuth tokens for each connection.  Mode 0600.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/summary.json
The session's model, status, totals, plan flag, schedules that
are still waiting, its todo list, and its latest review.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/messages.jsonl
The transcript, one JSON object per line.  A finding from another
live session is one of those lines.  @xref{Messages}.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/plan.md
The plan, when plan mode has written one.

@item sessions/_detached/@var{id}/
A chat with no project.

@item tasks/@var{id}.json
One headless task.  @xref{Headless}.

@item teams/@var{rig}.json
One rig: its pods, its seats, its edges, the session occupying
each seat, the work each seat still holds, the text sent to
each seat, the rig's chatroom, a pace that walks context into
a seat, and a queue row of owned work that can be claimed,
noted, closed, handed to another seat, or parked on one wake.
The same file holds intake that nobody owns yet.  It also
holds a declared workflow.  Closing its current step opens
the next step.  It also holds watchdog jobs that remain after
Emacs restarts.  It also holds the recap written when a seat
is handed to a successor.  The seat keeps its name, its
edges, and its inbound work.  The same file also holds the
notice written when a seat near its context wall is compacted
on purpose, and the room line that tells the rest of the team.
The transcript summary is @kbd{M-x harmless-compact}.
@xref{Team}.

@item teams/snapshots/@var{rig}/@var{name}.json
A named snapshot of that rig.  The report says, per seat,
whether it resumed, was rebuilt, started fresh, is waiting on
a decision, or failed.  A fresh start is a choice.  Checking
the snapshot does not write.  Restoring writes the rig back
only when no seat failed.
@xref{Team}.

@item teams/images/@var{rig}/@var{name}.json
A captured seat.  The live rig is unchanged by the capture.
Starting a seat from it copies the role and the inbound work
and leaves the session empty.  Nothing is started.
@xref{Team}.

@item teams/packets/@var{rig}/@var{name}.json
What a seat knows.  The live rig is unchanged by the capture.
Applying it keeps the seat name and binds a session that is
already running, with a different runtime.
@xref{Team}.

@item worktrees/@var{repository}/@var{name}/
An isolated checkout of that repository.  @xref{Worktrees}.

@item memory/global/
@itemx memory/workspaces/@var{project}/
Notes.  @xref{Memory}.

@item computer/
Screenshots from @code{computer_screenshot}.  @xref{Computer use}.
@end table

@var{project} in a session path is the project directory encoded as one
file name, not the project name you see in the buffer.

The log is not a file.  It is the buffer @code{*harmless-log*}.
@xref{Trouble}.

@node Keys
@chapter Keys

@findex harmless-menu
@kindex h @r{(Harmless buffers)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-menu} opens a transient with the common commands.
You can also open it with @kbd{m} in the transcript or @kbd{C-c C-k}
in the prompt.

@table @kbd
@item n
New session.

@item C
Detached chat.

@item c
Current project.

@item s
Switch session.

@item d
Dashboard.

@item a
Abort.

@item k
Compact the transcript.

